'''Hybe Co., Ltd.'''<ref name="Company established">{{Cite web|title=Company Information Popup|url=https://englishdart.fss.or.kr/dsbc001/selectPopup.ax?selectKey=01204056|website=englishdart.fss.or.kr|access-date=February 7, 2026|archive-date=October 6, 2025|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20251006082638/https://englishdart.fss.or.kr/dsbc001/selectPopup.ax?selectKey=01204056|url-status=live}}</ref> ({{korean|hangul=주식회사 하이브}}), commonly known as simply '''Hybe''' ({{korean|hangul=하이브}}; stylized in [[all caps]]), is a South Korean [[Multinational corporation|multinational]] conglomerate.<ref>{{Cite web|title=Hybe, the K-pop giant housing BTS, has become the first Korean entertainment firm to be designated a conglomerate|url=https://m.koreaherald.com/article/3394101|website=[[The Korea Herald]]|date=January 24, 2025|access-date=May 14, 2024}}</ref> Founded as Big Hit Entertainment, Hybe has grown from a traditional music entertainment company into a tech-driven lifestyle platform corporation.

==History==
===2005–2021: Big Hit Entertainment Co., Ltd.===
Big Hit Entertainment was founded on February 1, 2005,<ref>{{cite news|script-title=ko:'방탄소년단' 만든 이 남자의 헉소리 나는 히트곡들|trans-title='BTS', the man who made these hit songs|url=https://entertain.naver.com/read?oid=005&aid=0001053316|via=[[Naver]]|work=Kookmin Daily|language=ko|last=<!--Staff|first=writers; no by-line-->|date=December 6, 2017|access-date=June 22, 2020|archive-date=July 21, 2018|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180721043925/https://entertain.naver.com/read?oid=005&aid=0001053316|url-status=live}}</ref> and signed the vocal trio [[8Eight]] in 2007.<ref name=":0">{{cite web|script-title=ko:[단독]에이트, 7년만에 잠정적 해체.."각자 음악 활동"|trans-title=[Exclusive] 8Eight, temporarily disbanded after seven years.."their own music activities"|url=https://entertain.naver.com/read?oid=108&aid=0002369958|via=[[Naver]]|work=[[Star News (South Korea)|Star News]]|language=ko|last=Yoon|first=Sung-yeol|date=February 21, 2014|access-date=June 22, 2020|archive-date=June 22, 2020|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200622202440/https://entertain.naver.com/read?oid=108&aid=0002369958|url-status=live}}</ref> In 2010, the company signed a contract with [[JYP Entertainment]] to jointly manage the boy group [[2AM (band)|2AM]].<ref name=":2">{{cite news|title=2AM's Jo Kwon, Im Seulong, and Jung Jin Woon Return to JYP Entertainment|url=http://mwave.interest.me/enewsworld/en/article/63193/2am-changes-agencies|via=[[Mnet (TV channel)|Mwave]]|work=Newsen|last1=Lee|first1=Min-ji|last2=Danbi Hong|first2=Grace|date=April 9, 2014|access-date=June 22, 2020|archive-date=March 12, 2016|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160312112024/http://mwave.interest.me/enewsworld/en/article/63193/2am-changes-agencies|url-status=dead}}</ref> That year, Bang Si-hyuk signed [[RM (rapper)|RM]] as the first member of [[BTS]] and launched nationwide auditions to recruit other members for the group.<ref>{{cite magazine|script-title=ko:세상의 총알을 막아내는 소년들 그룹 방탄소년단.|trans-title=Group BTS, boys who block the world's bullets|url=http://www.cuvismmag.com/b/interview/2922|magazine=Cuvism Magazine|language=ko|last=<!--Staff|first=writers; no by-line-->|date=July 22, 2013|access-date=June 22, 2020|archive-date=June 6, 2015|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150606161844/http://cuvismmag.com/b/interview/2922|url-status=usurped}}</ref> BTS made their debut under Big Hit on June 13, 2013.<ref>{{cite web|title=[Video] BTS Asks about Your Dreams in 'No More Dream' MV|url=http://mwave.interest.me/enewsworld/en/article/38336/bts-releases-debut-video|work=[[Mnet (TV channel)|Mwave]]|last=Danbi Hong|first=Grace|date=June 12, 2013|access-date=June 22, 2020|archive-date=February 5, 2015|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150205172617/http://mwave.interest.me/enewsworld/en/article/38336/bts-releases-debut-video|url-status=dead}}</ref>

In 2012, the company signed [[Lim Jeong-hee]],<ref name=":1">{{cite news|title=Big Hit Reveals Lim Jeong Hee′s Contract Ended in May|url=http://mwave.interest.me/enewsworld/en/article/95932/big-hit-reveals-lim-jeong-hees-contract-ended-in-may|via=[[Mnet (TV channel)|Mwave]]|work=Newsen|last=Hwang|first=Hye-jin|date=June 12, 2013|access-date=June 22, 2020|archive-date=December 30, 2015|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20151230062103/http://mwave.interest.me/enewsworld/en/article/95932/big-hit-reveals-lim-jeong-hees-contract-ended-in-may|url-status=dead}}</ref> and formed the girl group [[GLAM (band)|Glam]] as a collaboration with [[Source Music]].<ref>{{cite news|script-title=ko:이병헌 협박한 가수 소속된 걸그룹 글램 해체|trans-title=The girl group Glam, whose singer threatened Lee Byung-hun, was disbanded|url=http://www.yonhapnews.co.kr/bulletin/2015/01/15/0200000000AKR20150115163200005.HTML|agency=[[Yonhap News Agency|Yonhap News]]|language=ko|last=Lee|first=Eun-jeong|date=January 15, 2015|access-date=October 3, 2018|archive-date=February 25, 2015|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150225002915/http://www.yonhapnews.co.kr/bulletin/2015/01/15/0200000000AKR20150115163200005.HTML|url-status=live}}</ref> The group was active until 2014, when it was disbanded due to legal actions against one of its members Kim Da-hee. Kim was sentenced to prison after being found guilty of blackmailing actor [[Lee Byung-hun]].<ref>{{cite news|script-title=ko:글램, 결국 3년 만에 해체...'이병헌 협박女' 다희-이지연 실형 선고|trans-title=Glam, finally disbanded after three years...'Lee Byung-hun blackmailed' Da-hee & Lee Ji-yeon sentenced to imprisonment|url=http://sports.chosun.com/news/utype.htm?id=201501160100179660010908&ServiceDate=20150115|work=[[Sports Chosun]]|language=ko|last=<!--Staff|first=writers; no by-line-->|date=January 15, 2015|access-date=June 22, 2020|archive-date=August 8, 2016|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160808012644/http://sports.chosun.com/news/utype.htm?id=201501160100179660010908&ServiceDate=20150115|url-status=dead}}</ref><ref>{{cite news|script-title=ko:글램 다희, 모델 친구와 음담패설 영상 유포 협박 "세계적 스타라 거액 요구" 이병헌 공식입장은?|trans-title=Glam's Da-hee threatens to spread video with model friend, "demands large sum of money from world star". What is Lee Byung-hun's official position?|url=http://sports.chosun.com/news/utype.htm?id=201409030100028830001520&ServiceDate=20140902|work=[[Sports Chosun]]|language=ko|last=<!--Staff|first=writers; no by-line-->|date=September 2, 2014|access-date=June 22, 2020|archive-date=August 8, 2016|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160808020934/http://sports.chosun.com/news/utype.htm?id=201409030100028830001520&ServiceDate=20140902|url-status=dead}}</ref><ref>{{cite news|title=Lee Byung-hun repeats apology upon returning home|url=http://kpopherald.koreaherald.com/view.php?ud=201502261617272907645_2|work=[[The Korea Herald]]|last=<!--Staff|first=writers; no by-line-->|date=February 26, 2015|access-date=June 22, 2020|archive-date=April 2, 2015|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150402172354/http://kpopherald.koreaherald.com/view.php?ud=201502261617272907645_2|url-status=live}}</ref>

Following the end of the joint contract between Big Hit and JYP in April 2014, three members of 2AM returned to JYP. [[Lee Chang-min (singer)|Lee Chang-min]] remained with Big Hit to continue with his solo career and as part of the duo [[Homme (duo)|Homme]].<ref name=":2" /> 8Eight disbanded later that year, after Baek Chan and Joo Hee's contracts with Big Hit ended.<ref name=":0" />

In May 2015, Lim parted ways with the agency, following the expiration of her three-year contract,<ref name=":1" /> and [[Signal Entertainment Group]], a [[KOSDAQ]]-listed company specializing in artist management and television production, acquired stakes in Big Hit through a [[South Korean won|KR₩]]6 billion [[convertible bond]].<ref name="edaily">{{cite news|script-title=ko:씨그널엔터테인먼트그룹 "빅히트엔터 CB 60억 인수"|trans-title=Signal Entertainment Group "Big Hit Entertainment 6 billion CB acquisition"|url=https://www.edaily.co.kr/news/read?newsId=03450566609368920&mediaCodeNo=257|work=[[Edaily]]|language=ko|last=Lee|first=Byung-Young|date=May 14, 2015|access-date=February 25, 2021|archive-date=August 20, 2016|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160820111920/http://www.edaily.co.kr/news/NewsRead.edy?SCD=JB31&newsid=03450566609368920&DCD=A10103&OutLnkChk=Y|url-status=live}}</ref> In early 2016, Big Hit ended their stake relationship with Signal and made a full settlement of the bonds.<ref>{{cite news|script-title=ko:빅히트엔터, 영업이익 100억 돌파...VC '흐뭇'|trans-title=Big Hit Entertainment, operating profit surpasses 10 billion...VC 'Happy'|url=http://m.thebell.co.kr/m/newsview.asp?svccode=00&newskey=201702090100018900001148|work=The Bell News|language=ko|last=Jung|first=Kang-hoon|date=February 14, 2017|access-date=June 22, 2020|archive-date=June 22, 2020|archive-url=https://archive.today/20200622221020/http://m.thebell.co.kr/m/newsview.asp?svccode=00&newskey=201702090100018900001148|url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{cite news|title=Signal Entertainment Group to sell entire shares of entertainment firm for 6.2 bln won|url=https://www.reuters.com/finance/stocks/099830.KQ/key-developments/article/3449988|work=[[Reuters]]|date=October 6, 2016|access-date=August 3, 2019|archive-date=March 23, 2017|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20170323085540/https://www.reuters.com/finance/stocks/099830.KQ/key-developments/article/3449988|url-status=dead}}</ref>

In February 2018, Homme disbanded after member Changmin's contract ended. He left the company to start his own agency, while [[Lee Hyun]] continued on as a solo artist.<ref name="Lee Hyun now solo">{{cite news|script-title=ko:[공식입장] 이창민, 빅히트와 전속계약 만료 "1인 기획사 설립"|trans-title=[Official Statement] Lee Changmin's contract with Bighit Entertainment expires, "will start his own agency"|url=http://entertain.naver.com/now/read?oid=311&aid=0000818920|via=[[Naver]]|work=Xports News|language=ko|last=Park|first=Sohyun|date=February 1, 2018|access-date=June 22, 2020|archive-date=February 1, 2018|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180201110641/https://entertain.naver.com/now/read?oid=311&aid=0000818920|url-status=live}}</ref> In August, Big Hit and [[CJ ENM Entertainment Division|CJ ENM]] released information stating plans to create a joint entertainment agency, with ownership split 52% to the latter and 48% to the former.<ref>{{cite news|title=BTS' management agency teams up with CJ affiliate to launch entertainment firm|url=http://english.yonhapnews.co.kr/business/2018/07/31/0501000000AEN20180731008500315.html|work=[[Yonhap News Agency|Yonhap News]]|last=Park|first=Bo-ram|date=July 31, 2018|access-date=June 22, 2020|archive-date=August 17, 2018|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180817220317/http://english.yonhapnews.co.kr/business/2018/07/31/0501000000AEN20180731008500315.html|url-status=dead}}</ref><ref>{{cite news|title=Entertainment Giants Team up to Manufacture 'Next BTS'|url=http://english.chosun.com/site/data/html_dir/2019/03/12/2019031201432.html|work=[[The Chosun Ilbo]]|last=Kim|first=Bong-kee|date=March 12, 2019|access-date=June 22, 2020|archive-date=March 12, 2019|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190312184147/http://english.chosun.com/site/data/html_dir/2019/03/12/2019031201432.html|url-status=live}}</ref> Filed under the name Belift Lab, the agency debuted its first boy group [[Enhypen]] in November 2020.<ref>{{cite news|title='Global fan-made band' Enhypen debuts with album 'Border: Day One'|url=https://koreajoongangdaily.joins.com/2020/11/30/entertainment/kpop/Enhypen-Belift-Lab-BTS/20201130185300522.html|work=[[Korea JoongAng Daily]]|last=Yoon|first=So-yeon|date=November 30, 2020|access-date=March 31, 2022|archive-date=November 30, 2020|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20201130141936/https://koreajoongangdaily.joins.com/2020/11/30/entertainment/kpop/Enhypen-Belift-Lab-BTS/20201130185300522.html|url-status=live}}</ref> In October, BTS renewed and extended their contract with the agency for seven more years.<ref>{{cite magazine|title=BTS Extend Contracts With Big Hit Entertainment Until 2026|url=https://www.forbes.com/sites/tamarherman/2018/10/17/bts-extend-contracts-with-big-hit-entertainment-until-2026/|magazine=[[Forbes]]|last=Herman|first=Tamar|date=October 17, 2018|access-date=June 22, 2020|archive-date=October 19, 2018|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20181019123114/https://www.forbes.com/sites/tamarherman/2018/10/17/bts-extend-contracts-with-big-hit-entertainment-until-2026/|url-status=live}}</ref> Big Hit was voted Best Investment Company of the Year at the 2018 Korea VC Awards in December.<ref>{{cite news|script-title=ko:한국벤처투자, 'Korea VC Awards 2018' 개최|trans-title=Korea Venture Investment host 'Korea VC Awards 2018'|url=https://news.naver.com/main/read.nhn?mode=LSD&mid=sec&oid=008&aid=0004143145&sid1=001|via=[[Naver|Naver News]]|work=[[Money Today]]|language=ko|last=Lee|first=Sang-yeon|date=December 6, 2018|access-date=June 22, 2020|archive-date=June 22, 2020|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200622223449/https://news.naver.com/main/read.nhn?mode=LSD&mid=sec&oid=008&aid=0004143145&sid1=001|url-status=live}}</ref>

Big Hit debuted its second male group, [[TXT (band)|Tomorrow X Together]] (TXT), in March 2019.<ref>{{cite magazine|title=BTS's Label Reveals Tomorrow X Together Will Debut March 4|url=https://www.forbes.com/sites/caitlinkelley/2019/02/06/bts-bighit-tomorrow-x-together-txt-debut/|magazine=[[Forbes]]|last=Kelley|first=Caitlin|date=February 6, 2019|access-date=June 22, 2020|archive-date=February 7, 2019|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190207125424/https://www.forbes.com/sites/caitlinkelley/2019/02/06/bts-bighit-tomorrow-x-together-txt-debut/|url-status=live}}</ref> Also that month, former [[Chief business officer|CBO]] Lenzo Yoon was appointed co-CEO alongside Bang.<ref>{{cite magazine|title=Big Hit Entertainment Appoints New Co-CEO Following Rise Of BTS, Launch Of TXT|url=https://www.forbes.com/sites/tamarherman/2019/03/07/big-hit-entertainment-appoints-new-co-ceo-following-rise-of-bts-launch-of-txt/|magazine=[[Forbes]]|last=Herman|first=Tamar|date=March 7, 2019|access-date=June 22, 2020|archive-date=December 7, 2019|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20191207032822/https://www.forbes.com/sites/tamarherman/2019/03/07/big-hit-entertainment-appoints-new-co-ceo-following-rise-of-bts-launch-of-txt/|url-status=live}}</ref> Yoon would focus on the business components of the company, while Bang's focus would be on creative production.<ref>{{cite web|script-title=ko:빅히트 '방탄 성장 주역' 윤석준 공동 대표이사 선임|trans-title=Big Hit 'Ballistic Growth Leader' Yoon Seok-jun appointed as co-CEO|url=https://entertain.naver.com/read?oid=108&aid=0002765749|via=[[Naver]]|work=[[Star News (South Korea)|Star News]]|language=ko|last=Lee|first=Jung-ho|date=March 6, 2019|access-date=August 3, 2019|archive-date=March 6, 2019|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190306111759/https://entertain.naver.com/read?oid=108&aid=0002765749|url-status=live}}</ref> In July 2019, the company acquired [[Source Music]],<ref>{{cite magazine|title=Big Hit Entertainment, BTS's Label, Acquires Source Music|url=https://www.forbes.com/sites/caitlinkelley/2019/07/28/btss-label-big-hit-entertainment-acquired-source-music/|magazine=[[Forbes]]|last=Kelley|first=Caitlin|date=July 28, 2019|access-date=June 22, 2020|archive-date=October 11, 2019|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20191011220608/https://www.forbes.com/sites/caitlinkelley/2019/07/28/btss-label-big-hit-entertainment-acquired-source-music/|url-status=live}}</ref> and in August, the video game firm Superb.<ref>{{cite news|script-title=ko:빅히트, 음악게임 회사 수퍼브 인수..방시혁 "긍정적 가치 가져다줄 것"[공식]|trans-title=[Official] Bighit acquires music game company Superb..Bang Sihyuk "It will bring positive value"|url=https://entertain.naver.com/now/read?oid=109&aid=0004070079|via=[[Naver]]|work=Osen|language=ko|last=Kim|first=Eun-ae|date=August 19, 2018|access-date=June 22, 2020|archive-date=June 22, 2020|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200622230326/https://entertain.naver.com/now/read?oid=109&aid=0004070079|url-status=live}}</ref> Thanks to the [[Weverse]] and Weverse Shop{{Efn|formerly Weply prior to March 9, 2020<ref>{{cite tweet|user=weverseshop|number=1234403722228187136|date=March 2, 2020|title=From 9 March 2020, the global fan merch app, Weply is changing its name to Weverse Shop! All your Weply account information will remain the same on Weverse Shop. You can shop your favorite merch without having to worry about a thing! More info here! 👉|link=no|access-date=June 22, 2020|archive-date=May 20, 2024|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240520221412/https://x.com/weverseshop/status/1234403722228187136|url-status=live}}</ref>}} apps developed by its subsidiary beNX, Big Hit was named the fourth most innovative company of 2020 worldwide by ''[[Fast Company]]''.<ref>{{cite magazine|title=Millions of BTS fans use these 2 apps to connect and shop. No tech startups needed|url=https://www.fastcompany.com/90457458/big-hit-entertainment-most-innovative-companies-2020|magazine=[[Fast Company]]|last=Mehta|first=Stephanie|date=March 10, 2020|access-date=June 22, 2020|archive-date=March 10, 2020|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200310155119/https://www.fastcompany.com/90457458/big-hit-entertainment-most-innovative-companies-2020|url-status=live}}</ref>

In May 2020, Big Hit became the majority shareholder of [[Pledis Entertainment]]. The label would retain its independence; however, its artists would now receive greater financial support and global promotions.<ref>{{cite magazine|title=BTS's Agency Big Hit Becomes Majority Shareholder Of K-Pop Company Pledis, Home To Seventeen & NU'EST|url=https://www.forbes.com/sites/tamarherman/2020/05/24/btss-agency-big-hit-becomes-majority-shareholder-of-k-pop-company-pledis-home-to-seventeen--nuest|magazine=[[Forbes (magazine)|Forbes]]|last=Herman|first=Tamar|date=May 25, 2020|access-date=June 22, 2020|archive-date=June 2, 2020|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200602124840/https://www.forbes.com/sites/tamarherman/2020/05/24/btss-agency-big-hit-becomes-majority-shareholder-of-k-pop-company-pledis-home-to-seventeen--nuest/#7453b0855766|url-status=live}}</ref> The [[Fair Trade Commission (South Korea)|Fair Trade Commission]] (KFTC) approved the acquisition in October.<ref>{{cite news|script-title=ko:이제 진짜 한솥밥 식구...공정위, BTS·세븐틴 소속사 결합 승인|trans-title=Now we're really in the same pot. Fair Trade Commission approves BTS and Seventeen's agency combination|url=https://news.naver.com/main/read.nhn?mode=LSD&mid=sec&oid=421&aid=0004933137&sid1=001|via=[[Naver|Naver News]]|work=News1|language=ko|last=Kim|first=Seong|date=October 18, 2020|access-date=February 17, 2021|archive-date=February 18, 2021|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210218024215/https://news.naver.com/main/read.nhn?mode=LSD&mid=sec&oid=421&aid=0004933137&sid1=001|url-status=live}}</ref> Big Hit acquired KOZ Entertainment, a record label founded by rapper [[Zico (rapper)|Zico]], the following month.<ref name="Benjamin">{{cite magazine|title=Big Hit Entertainment To Acquire Label Founded By Korean Super Producer-Rapper Zico|url=https://www.forbes.com/sites/jeffbenjamin/2020/11/18/big-hit-entertainment-to-acquire-koz-entertainment-founded-by-korean-super-producerrapper-zico/|magazine=[[Forbes]]|last=Benjamin|first=Jeff|date=November 18, 2020|access-date=November 19, 2020|archive-date=November 18, 2020|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20201118075511/https://www.forbes.com/sites/jeffbenjamin/2020/11/18/big-hit-entertainment-to-acquire-koz-entertainment-founded-by-korean-super-producerrapper-zico/|url-status=live}}</ref>

In January 2021, news media reported that Big Hit and beNX had invested a combined ₩70 billion (US$63 million) in [[YG Plus]], acquiring a 17.9% stake in the company in a merchandising and distribution deal that would see YG Plus' artists join the Weverse social media and shop platforms.<ref>{{cite magazine|title=BTS Agency Big Hit Entertainment to Invest $63 Million in Rival YG|url=https://variety.com/2021/biz/asia/bts-agency-big-hit-entertainment-invest-in-yg-1234893216/|magazine=[[Variety (magazine)|Variety]]|last=Frater|first=Patrick|date=January 27, 2021|access-date=February 17, 2021|archive-date=January 27, 2021|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210127115651/https://variety.com/2021/biz/asia/bts-agency-big-hit-entertainment-invest-in-yg-1234893216/|url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{cite magazine|title=BTS's Label Big Hit Now Owns Part of Rival K-Pop Company YG|url=https://www.rollingstone.com/pro/news/bts-big-hit-yg-kpop-1119705/|magazine=[[Rolling Stone]]|last=Stassen|first=Murray|date=January 27, 2021|access-date=February 11, 2021|archive-date=January 27, 2021|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210127165018/https://www.rollingstone.com/pro/news/bts-big-hit-yg-kpop-1119705/|url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{cite news|title=Big Hit partnership with Naver, YG Plus a win-win for entertainment industry|url=http://www.koreaherald.com/view.php?ud=20210131000171|work=[[The Korea Herald]]|last=Choi|first=Ji-won|date=January 31, 2021|access-date=February 25, 2021|archive-date=January 31, 2021|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210131075813/http://www.koreaherald.com/view.php?ud=20210131000171|url-status=live}}</ref> [[Naver Corporation]] later invested ₩354.8 billion ($321 million) in beNX, acquiring 49% of the subsidiary. In return, it transferred its [[V Live]] video streaming service to the latter for the development of a new integrated fan community platform. BeNX was eventually renamed Weverse Company.<ref name="KJAD Naver benx">{{cite news|title=Big Hit, Naver and YG join forces in a big K-pop stock swap|url=https://koreajoongangdaily.joins.com/2021/01/27/business/industry/Big-Hit-Entertainment-beNX-YG-Plus/20210127192300544.html|work=[[Korea JoongAng Daily]]|last=Yoon|first=Zo-yeon|date=January 27, 2021|access-date=February 17, 2021|archive-date=January 27, 2021|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210127111710/https://koreajoongangdaily.joins.com/2021/01/27/business/industry/Big-Hit-Entertainment-beNX-YG-Plus/20210127192300544.html|url-status=live}}</ref> The merger was approved by the KFTC in May.<ref>{{cite news|title=FTC gives go ahead for merger deal between V Live and Weverse|url=http://www.koreaherald.com/view.php?ud=20210513000792|work=[[The Korea Herald]]|last=Kim|first=Hyun-su|date=May 13, 2021|access-date=June 9, 2021|archive-date=May 13, 2021|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210513062611/http://www.koreaherald.com/view.php?ud=20210513000792|url-status=live}}</ref> On February 17, Big Hit and [[Universal Music Group]] (UMG) announced a strategic partnership between the two companies to collaborate on various music and technology-related endeavors.<ref>{{cite magazine|title=Big Hit Entertainment & Universal Music Group Detail Partnership For Boy Band, Technology Offerings|url=https://www.forbes.com/sites/jeffbenjamin/2021/02/17/big-hit-entertainment--universal-music-detail-expanded-partnership-for-boy-band-tech-offerings/|magazine=[[Forbes]]|last=Benjamin|first=Jeff|date=February 17, 2021|access-date=February 18, 2021|archive-date=February 18, 2021|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210218001109/https://www.forbes.com/sites/jeffbenjamin/2021/02/17/big-hit-entertainment--universal-music-detail-expanded-partnership-for-boy-band-tech-offerings/|url-status=live}}</ref> At the forefront of the partnership was a joint venture between Big Hit and [[Interscope Geffen A&M Records|Geffen Records]], a UMG flagship label, that will debut a global boy group through a new, [[Los Angeles]]-based label following a global audition program set to air in 2022. Big Hit would be responsible for selecting and training the artists, while UMG would be in charge of music production, global distribution, and marketing.<ref>{{cite magazine|title=Big Hit Entertainment, Home of BTS, and Universal Music Unveil Expanded Partnership, Including New Label|url=https://variety.com/2021/music/news/big-hit-bts-universal-music-1234909832/|magazine=[[Variety (magazine)|Variety]]|last=Aswad|first=Jem|date=February 17, 2021|access-date=February 18, 2021|archive-date=February 23, 2021|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210223210529/https://variety.com/2021/music/news/big-hit-bts-universal-music-1234909832/|url-status=live}}</ref> Additionally, other UMG artists were set to join Weverse, already home to [[Gracie Abrams]], [[New Hope Club]], and Alexander 23.<ref>{{cite magazine|title=Universal Music Group and Big Hit Records Partnering to Debut New Boy Group|url=https://www.hollywoodreporter.com/news/universal-music-group-and-big-hit-records-partnering-to-debut-new-boy-group|magazine=[[The Hollywood Reporter]]|last1=Perez|first1=Lexy|last2=Sun|first2=Rebecca|date=February 17, 2021|access-date=February 18, 2021|archive-date=February 18, 2021|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210218000745/https://www.hollywoodreporter.com/news/universal-music-group-and-big-hit-records-partnering-to-debut-new-boy-group|url-status=live}}</ref> On February 25, Big Hit announced a ₩4 billion ($3.6 million) investment in a Korea-based AI company, Supertone, that specializes in creating hyper-realistic voices using technology.<ref>{{cite web|title=Big Hit invests $3.6m in AI fake voice startup. Could it clone BTS's vocals?|url=https://www.musicbusinessworldwide.com/big-hit-invests-3-6m-in-supertone-an-ai-firm-that-just-cloned-a-dead-superstars-voice/|website=[[Music Business Worldwide]]|last=Murray|first=Stassen|date=February 25, 2021|access-date=February 26, 2021|archive-date=February 25, 2021|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210225235449/https://www.musicbusinessworldwide.com/big-hit-invests-3-6m-in-supertone-an-ai-firm-that-just-cloned-a-dead-superstars-voice/|url-status=live}}</ref>

===2021–present: Hybe, expansions, and investigations===
Big Hit announced its rebranding into an entertainment lifestyle platform company under the name Hybe in March 2021.<ref>{{cite news|script-title=ko:빅히트, 하이브(HYBE)로 사명 바꾼다...방시혁 의장 사내이사 후보|trans-title=|url=https://entertain.naver.com/read?oid=609&aid=0000411584|via=[[Naver]]|work=Newsen|language=ko|last=Hwang|first=Hye-jin|date=March 11, 2021|access-date=March 19, 2021|archive-date=March 20, 2021|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210320003654/https://entertain.naver.com/read?oid=609&aid=0000411584|url-status=live}}</ref> The company underwent a complete organizational restructuring, which also saw the recording and management arm of Big Hit Entertainment spun off into an independent label named [[BigHit Music]] under Hybe's new Labels division. The renaming was approved at a shareholders meeting on March 30.<ref name="hybe presentation">{{cite news|title=[Newsmaker] Big Hit Entertainment to change name to Hybe|url=http://www.koreaherald.com/view.php?ud=20210319000808|work=[[The Korea Herald]]|last=Choi|first=Ji-won|date=March 19, 2021|access-date=March 19, 2021|archive-date=March 19, 2021|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210319071054/http://www.koreaherald.com/view.php?ud=20210319000808|url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{cite web|title=BTS' agency Big Hit Entertainment rebrands itself as HYBE|url=https://www.nme.com/en_asia/news/music/bts-agency-big-hit-entertainment-rebrands-itself-as-hybe-2903910|website=[[NME]]|last=Yeo|first=Gladys|date=March 19, 2021|access-date=March 19, 2021|archive-date=March 19, 2021|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210319085421/https://www.nme.com/en_asia/news/music/bts-agency-big-hit-entertainment-rebrands-itself-as-hybe-2903910|url-status=live}}</ref> The company relocated to its new headquarters in the Yongsan Trade Center in [[Yongsan District]] on March 22,<ref name="hybe presentation" /><ref name="yongsan">{{cite news|script-title=ko:빅히트 사옥 이전, 용산으로 [공식]|trans-title=Big Hit office relocated to Yongsan [Official]|url=https://sports.donga.com/article/all/20210322/106009144/1|work=[[Sports Donga]]|language=ko|last=Jeong|first=Hee-yeon|date=March 22, 2021|access-date=March 28, 2021|archive-date=March 28, 2021|archive-url=https://archive.today/20210328205228/https://sports.donga.com/article/all/20210322/106009144/1|url-status=live}}</ref> and the rebrand went into effect on March 31.<ref>{{cite news|script-title=ko:'하이브' 이름 바꾼 빅히트, 투자 전문가 박영호 사외이사 선임|trans-title=Big Hit, renamed 'Hybe', appoints investment expert Park Young-ho as outside director|url=https://biz.chosun.com/site/data/html_dir/2021/03/30/2021033002436.html|work=[[The Chosun Ilbo]]|language=ko|last=Jung|first=Min-ha|date=March 30, 2021|access-date=March 31, 2021|archive-date=March 31, 2021|archive-url=https://archive.today/20210331031919/https://biz.chosun.com/site/data/html_dir/2021/03/30/2021033002436.html|url-status=live}}</ref> Hybe acquired [[Scooter Braun]]'s Ithaca Holdings and all its properties, including SB Projects{{Efn|manages artists such as [[Justin Bieber]] and [[Ariana Grande]]}} and [[Big Machine Records#Imprints|Big Machine Label Group]], through its subsidiary Hybe America{{Efn|name=Hybe America|Though corporate filing documentation uses the name "Big Hit America" it is Hybe America being referenced. News outlets interchangeably use "Big Hit America" and "Hybe America" to refer to the company acquiring Ithaca for Hybe Corp, indicating that they are one and the same. This article hereinafter uses Hybe America only.}} in April.<ref name="Variety Ithaca">{{cite magazine|title=HYBE, Formerly Big Hit Entertainment, Merges With Scooter Braun's Ithaca Holdings Bringing Together BTS, Justin Bieber, Big Machine (EXCLUSIVE)|url=https://variety.com/2021/digital/news/hybe-formerly-big-hit-entertainment-acquires-scooter-brauns-ithaca-holdings-bringing-together-bts-justin-bieber-big-machine-1234943092/|magazine=[[Variety (magazine)|Variety]]|last1=Halperin|first1=Shirley|last2=Frater|first2=Patrick|date=April 2, 2021|access-date=April 2, 2021|archive-date=April 2, 2021|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210402075231/https://variety.com/2021/digital/news/hybe-formerly-big-hit-entertainment-acquires-scooter-brauns-ithaca-holdings-bringing-together-bts-justin-bieber-big-machine-1234943092/|url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{cite magazine|title=Entertainment Giant HYBE, Home of BTS, Purchases Scooter Braun's Ithaca Holdings|url=https://www.rollingstone.com/pro/news/scooter-braun-ithaca-holdings-hybe-purchase-merger-1150462/|magazine=[[Rolling Stone]]|last=Blistein|first=Jon|date=April 2, 2021|access-date=April 4, 2021|archive-date=April 2, 2021|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210402135721/https://www.rollingstone.com/pro/news/scooter-braun-ithaca-holdings-hybe-purchase-merger-1150462/|url-status=live}}</ref> The corporation invested $950&nbsp;million (₩1.07&nbsp;trillion) into the America branch to finance the buyout;<ref>{{cite web|title=Scooter Braun Sells Ithaca Holdings To South Korea's HYBE, Home Of BTS, In $1 Billion Deal|url=https://deadline.com/2021/04/scooter-braun-bts-justin-bieber-ariana-grande-ithaca-holdings-hybe-south-korea-1234726736/|website=[[Deadline Hollywood]]|last=Hayes|first=Dade|date=April 2, 2021|access-date=April 4, 2021|archive-date=April 2, 2021|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210402163139/https://deadline.com/2021/04/scooter-braun-bts-justin-bieber-ariana-grande-ithaca-holdings-hybe-south-korea-1234726736/|url-status=live}}</ref> $1.05&nbsp;billion was paid to Ithaca shareholders and bondholders.<ref>{{cite news|title=Scooter Braun Sells Ithaca Holdings To South Korea's HYBE, Home Of BTS, In $1 Billion Deal|url=https://www.bloomberg.com/news/articles/2021-04-02/k-pop-agency-to-buy-justin-bieber-manager-s-firm-for-1-billion|work=[[Bloomberg News]]|last1=Kang|first1=Shinhye|last2=Lee|first2=Heesu|date=April 2, 2021|access-date=April 4, 2021|archive-date=April 2, 2021|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210402092943/https://www.bloomberg.com/news/articles/2021-04-02/k-pop-agency-to-buy-justin-bieber-manager-s-firm-for-1-billion|url-status=live}}</ref> BH Odyssey Merger Sub, a newly created subsidiary of Hybe America, facilitated the acquisition, and was later dissolved after the deal's finalization.<ref name="KJAD Ithaca">{{cite news|title=HYBE takes over Big Hit America, will also merge with Ithaca Holdings|url=https://koreajoongangdaily.joins.com/2021/04/02/entertainment/kpop/HYBE-Ithaca-Holdings-BTS/20210402164300390.html|work=[[Korea JoongAng Daily]]|last=Yoon|first=So-eon|date=April 2, 2021|access-date=April 2, 2021|archive-date=April 2, 2021|archive-url=https://archive.today/20210402205302/https://koreajoongangdaily.joins.com/2021/04/02/entertainment/kpop/HYBE-Ithaca-Holdings-BTS/20210402164300390.html|url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{cite magazine|title=South Korea's HYBE, Home of BTS, Acquires Scooter Braun's Ithaca Holdings|url=https://www.hollywoodreporter.com/news/south-koreas-hybe-home-of-bts-acquires-scooter-brauns-ithaca-holdings|magazine=The Hollywood Reporter|last=Brzeski|first=Patrick|date=April 2, 2021|access-date=April 4, 2021|archive-date=April 2, 2021|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210402100824/https://www.hollywoodreporter.com/news/south-koreas-hybe-home-of-bts-acquires-scooter-brauns-ithaca-holdings|url-status=live}}</ref> The Carlyle Group, a minority shareholder and original investor, sold its stake in Ithaca as part of the deal. Braun joined Hybe's board, while [[Scott Borchetta]] retained his position as CEO of Big Machine Label Group.<ref name="Variety Ithaca" /><ref>{{cite web|title=Scooter Braun Sells Ithaca Holdings To Hybe, Formerly Known As Big Hit Entertainment|url=https://www.musicbusinessworldwide.com/scooter-braun-sells-ithaca-holdings-to-hybe-formerly-big-hit-entertainment/|website=[[Music Business Worldwide]]|last=Ingham|first=Tim|date=April 2, 2021|access-date=April 2, 2021|archive-date=April 2, 2021|archive-url=https://archive.today/20210402093226/https://www.musicbusinessworldwide.com/scooter-braun-sells-ithaca-holdings-to-hybe-formerly-big-hit-entertainment/|url-status=live}}</ref> That same month, ''[[Time (magazine)|Time]]'' named Hybe one of the "100 Most Influential Companies of 2021", under the "Pioneers" section.<ref>{{cite magazine|title=2021 Time100 Most Influential Companies|url=https://time.com/collection/time100-companies/5953582/hybe/|magazine=[[Time (magazine)|Time]]|last=Bruner|first=Raisa|date=April 27, 2021|access-date=April 27, 2021|archive-date=April 27, 2021|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210427213713/https://time.com/collection/time100-companies/5953582/hybe/|url-status=live}}</ref>

Japanese streaming platform [[Showroom (streaming service)|Showroom]] announced a business and capital partnership with Hybe in May 2021 to improve its domestic services and expand access to Japanese content in South Korea, the United States, and globally.<ref>{{cite web|script-title=ja:SHOWROOMがBTSやSEVENTEENら所属HYBEと資本業務提携|trans-title=Showroom partners with BTS, Seventeen and other Hybe members in capital operations|url=https://natalie.mu/music/news/427537|website=[[Music Natalie]]|language=ko|last=<!--Staff|first=writers; no by-line-->|date=May 10, 2021|access-date=May 12, 2021|archive-date=May 10, 2021|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210510011910/https://natalie.mu/music/news/427537|url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{cite web|script-title=ja:SHOWROOM、BTS（防弾少年団）ら所属するHYBEと資本業務を提携...今後に注目|trans-title=Showroom, BTS, and other affiliated Hybe partners in capital business...focus on the future|url=https://news.kstyle.com/article.ksn?articleNo=2168127|website=Kstyle|language=ko|last=<!--Kstyle|first=editorial department-->|date=May 10, 2021|access-date=May 12, 2021|archive-date=May 10, 2021|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210510014012/https://news.kstyle.com/article.ksn?articleNo=2168127|url-status=live}}</ref> Hybe was added to the [[MSCI]] Korea index on May 12, and the index updated on May 28.<ref>{{cite news|script-title=ko:MSCI 지수 정기변경.. HMM·하이브·SKC·녹십자 등 4종목 편입|trans-title=Regular change of MSCI index.. 4 stocks HMM, Hybe, SKC, and Green Cross included.|url=https://news.naver.com/main/read.nhn?mode=LSD&mid=sec&sid1=101&oid=014&aid=0004637157|via=[[Naver|Naver News]]|work=[[The Financial News]]|language=ko|last=Seo|first=Hye-jin|date=May 12, 2021|access-date=May 12, 2021|archive-date=May 12, 2021|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210512050450/https://news.naver.com/main/read.nhn?mode=LSD&mid=sec&sid1=101&oid=014&aid=0004637157|url-status=live}}</ref> Following a second organizational restructuring, Bang resigned as CEO on July 1 to return his focus to music production. He was replaced by Jiwon Park but retained his position as chairman of the board of directors.<ref name="Bang resigns CEO">{{cite magazine|title=Bang Si-hyuk Steps Down as CEO of BTS Powerhouse HYBE|url=https://variety.com/2021/music/news/bang-si-hyuk-steps-down-as-ceo-of-bts-powerhouse-hybe-1235009779/|magazine=[[Variety (magazine)|Variety]]|last1=Aswad|first1=Jem|last2=Davis|first2=Rebecca|date=July 1, 2021|access-date=August 15, 2021|archive-date=July 4, 2021|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210704041948/https://variety.com/2021/music/news/bang-si-hyuk-steps-down-as-ceo-of-bts-powerhouse-hybe-1235009779/|url-status=live}}</ref><ref name="BSH resign MBW">{{cite web|title=Bang Si-hyuk Steps Down As CEO Of Hybe, Formerly Known As Big Hit Entertainment|url=https://www.musicbusinessworldwide.com/bang-si-hyuk-steps-down-as-ceo-of-hybe/|website=[[Music Business Worldwide]]|last=Stassen|first=Murray|date=July 1, 2021|access-date=August 15, 2021|archive-date=July 12, 2021|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210712141932/https://www.musicbusinessworldwide.com/bang-si-hyuk-steps-down-as-ceo-of-hybe/|url-status=live}}</ref> In November, Hybe entered into an equity-based partnership with Korean [[Financial technology|fintech]] company Dunamu to develop a joint venture [[Non-fungible token|NFT]] business for the creation of digital assets.<ref>{{cite news|title=HYBE sets its sights on webtoons, web novels, NFTs and more|url=https://koreajoongangdaily.joins.com/2021/11/04/entertainment/kpop/HYBE-brief-BTS/20211104161532237.html|work=[[Korea JoongAng Daily]]|last=Shin|first=Min-hee|date=November 4, 2021|access-date=November 4, 2021|archive-date=November 4, 2021|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20211104200024/https://koreajoongangdaily.joins.com/2021/11/04/entertainment/kpop/HYBE-brief-BTS/20211104161532237.html|url-status=live}}</ref><ref name="Pulse">{{cite news|title=Hybe and Dunamu enter equity-based partnership for NFT business|url=https://pulsenews.co.kr/view.php?year=2021&no=1046251|work=Pulse News|last=Cho|first=Jeehyun|date=November 4, 2021|access-date=November 5, 2021|archive-date=November 4, 2021|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20211104182314/https://pulsenews.co.kr/view.php?year=2021&no=1046251|url-status=live}}</ref> It acquired a 2.6% stake in the company for ₩500&nbsp;billion ($423.8&nbsp;million), while Dunamu received a 5.6% stake in Hybe for ₩700&nbsp;billion ($590&nbsp;million).<ref name="Pulse" /><ref>{{cite web|title=Get Ready For BTS NFTS: HYBE To Acquire $421M Stake In Crypto Firm And Launch Collectables JV|url=https://www.musicbusinessworldwide.com/get-ready-for-official-bts-nfts-as-hybe-acquires-421m-stake-in-crypto-firm-and-launches-digital-collectables-jv/|website=[[Music Business Worldwide]]|last=Stassen|first=Murray|date=November 4, 2021|access-date=November 5, 2021|archive-date=November 4, 2021|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20211104202453/https://www.musicbusinessworldwide.com/get-ready-for-official-bts-nfts-as-hybe-acquires-421m-stake-in-crypto-firm-and-launches-digital-collectables-jv/|url-status=live}}</ref> On November 11, Hybe won a [[Red Dot Design Award]] in the Brands & Communication Design category at the 2021 Red Dot Design Awards for its new [[corporate identity]] (CI) design.{{Efn|revealed in March by then-[[Chief brand officer|CBO]] Min Hee-jin during the rebrand announcement<ref name="hybe presentation" />}}<ref>{{cite web|script-title=ko:하이브 CI 2021 레드닷 디자인 어워드 본상 수상 영예[공식]|trans-title=Hybe CI 2021 Red Dot Design Award, Bonsang Award [Official]|url=https://entertain.naver.com/read?oid=108&aid=0003003774|via=[[Naver]]|work=[[Star News (South Korea)|Star News]]|last=Yoon|first=Sang-geun|date=November 11, 2021|access-date=November 10, 2021|archive-date=November 11, 2021|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20211111022734/https://entertain.naver.com/read?oid=108&aid=0003003774|url-status=live}}</ref> The establishment of a new music label, ADOR,{{Efn|acronym for "All doors, one room"<ref name="ADOR">{{cite news|title=HYBE to establish new music label with Min Hee-jin at helm|url=https://koreajoongangdaily.joins.com/2021/11/12/entertainment/kpop/HYBE-Min-Heejin-ADOR/20211112104121840.html|work=[[Korea JoongAng Daily]]|last=Lee|first=Jae-lim|date=November 12, 2021|access-date=October 9, 2022|archive-date=November 12, 2021|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20211112021825/https://koreajoongangdaily.joins.com/2021/11/12/entertainment/kpop/HYBE-Min-Heejin-ADOR/20211112104121840.html|url-status=live}}</ref>}} was announced on November 12. [[Chief brand officer]] (CBO) [[Min Hee-jin]] was appointed as its president and tasked with overseeing the management of the label's first girl group,{{Efn|members were selected through the "Plus Global Audition" held in 2019<ref name="ADOR" />}} later announced as [[NewJeans]], which debuted in July 2022.<ref>{{cite web|title=Hybe's latest girl group NewJeans make their much-anticipated debut with 'Attention'|url=https://www.nme.com/news/music/newjeans-attention-debut-single-music-video-ador-hybe-3274591|website=[[NME]]|last=Chin|first=Carmen|date=July 22, 2022|access-date=October 9, 2022|archive-date=July 22, 2022|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220722151331/https://www.nme.com/news/music/newjeans-attention-debut-single-music-video-ador-hybe-3274591|url-status=live}}</ref>

In January 2022, Hybe published a series of "original stories" featuring boy groups BTS, Enhypen, and Tomorrow X Together in collaboration with [[Webtoon (publisher)|Webtoon]] and [[Wattpad]] as part of a planned IP expansion.<ref>{{cite news|title=Hybe to premiere webtoon series featuring BTS this week|url=http://www.koreaherald.com/view.php?ud=20220112000772|work=[[The Korea Herald]]|last=Choi|first=Ji-won|date=January 12, 2022|access-date=March 31, 2022|archive-date=January 12, 2022|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220112071940/http://www.koreaherald.com/view.php?ud=20220112000772|url-status=live}}</ref> The BTS comic alone earned over 15 million views within two days of release, becoming the fastest title in Webtoon's history to reach that milestone.<ref>{{cite magazine|title=BTS Online Comic '7Fates: CHAKHO' Hits 15 Million Views – Global Bulletin|url=https://variety.com/2022/digital/global/bts-7fates-chakho-1235157245/|magazine=[[Variety (magazine)|Variety]]|last=Ramachandran|first=Naman|date=January 19, 2022|access-date=March 31, 2022|archive-date=January 20, 2022|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220120023859/https://variety.com/2022/digital/global/bts-7fates-chakho-1235157245/|url-status=live}}</ref> In March, ''Fast Company'' named Hybe the 11th most innovative company of the year globally on its annual top-50 list<ref>{{cite magazine|title=The World's 50 Most Innovative Companies 2022|url=https://www.fastcompany.com/most-innovative-companies/list|magazine=[[Fast Company]]|date=March 8, 2022|access-date=March 8, 2022|archive-date=March 8, 2022|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220308121010/https://www.fastcompany.com/most-innovative-companies/list|url-status=live}}</ref>—ranking first in the Media category<ref>{{cite magazine|title=The 10 most innovative media companies of 2022|url=https://www.fastcompany.com/90724442/most-innovative-companies-media-2022|magazine=[[Fast Company]]|last=Laporte|first=Nicole|date=March 8, 2022|access-date=March 8, 2022|archive-date=March 8, 2022|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220308112019/https://www.fastcompany.com/90724442/most-innovative-companies-media-2022|url-status=live}}</ref>—while ''Time'' included Hybe on its 100 Most Influential Companies list for a second consecutive year.<ref>{{cite magazine|title=Time100 Most Influential Companies of 2022|url=https://time.com/collection/time100-companies-2022/6159410/hybe-bang-si-hyuk-bts-interview/|magazine=[[Time (magazine)|Time]]|last=Bruner|first=Raisa|date=March 30, 2022|access-date=March 31, 2022|archive-date=March 30, 2022|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220330122205/https://time.com/collection/time100-companies-2022/6159410/hybe-bang-si-hyuk-bts-interview/|url-status=live}}</ref> The company topped the "Leaders" category and was dubbed "The Pop Powerhouse" by the outlet for its acquisitions, expansion into the digital frontier, and transformative strides in the music industry during the pandemic era.<ref>{{cite news|title=HYBE, Pinkfong listed on TIME's 100 Most Influential Companies|url=https://www.koreatimes.co.kr/www/art/2022/03/398_326527.html|work=[[The Korea Times]]|last=Lee|first=Gyu-lee|date=March 31, 2022|access-date=March 31, 2022|archive-date=March 31, 2022|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220331114253/https://www.koreatimes.co.kr/www/art/2022/03/398_326527.html|url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{cite web|title=BTS agency HYBE makes TIME 100 most influential companies list anew|url=https://www.gmanetwork.com/news/showbiz/showbizabroad/826881/bts-agency-hybe-makes-time-100-most-influential-companies-list-anew/story/|website=[[GMA Network|GMA News Online]]|date=March 30, 2022|access-date=March 31, 2022|archive-date=March 31, 2022|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220331110003/https://www.gmanetwork.com/news/showbiz/showbizabroad/826881/bts-agency-hybe-makes-time-100-most-influential-companies-list-anew/story/|url-status=live}}</ref> The following month, Hybe won an [[iF Product Design Award]] for "Company Branding" in the Communication category at the [[International Forum Design|International Forum Design Awards]] held in Germany.<ref>{{cite news|script-title=ko:하이브, 세계 3대 디자인상 2관왕 ...'iF 디자인 어워드 2022' 본상|trans-title=Hybe Wins Two of the World's Top Three Design Awards... "iF Design Awards 2022" Bonsang|url=https://n.news.naver.com/mnews/article/016/0001981286?sid=103|via=[[Naver]]|work=Herald Economy|last=Ko|first=Seung-hee|date=April 22, 2022|access-date=April 25, 2022|archive-date=April 22, 2022|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220422052408/https://n.news.naver.com/mnews/article/016/0001981286?sid=103|url-status=live}}</ref> Hybe signed a multi-year deal with [[List of assets owned by The Walt Disney Company#The Walt Disney Company Asia Pacific|The Walt Disney Company Asia Pacific]] in July 2022 to provide content showcasing the Korean music and entertainment industries to a global market. Five programs will be available worldwide on [[Disney+]] and other Disney streaming platforms—a [[4K resolution|4K]] cinematic film of BTS' [[Permission to Dance on Stage#Film|Permission to Dance on Stage concert in Los Angeles]]; ''[[In the Soop: Friendcation]]''; and ''BTS Monuments: Beyond the Star'', a [[Television documentary|docuseries]] of BTS' history from debut to present set to premiere in 2023. Additional content featuring other Hybe artists will also be released.<ref>{{cite magazine|title=Disney+ to Produce Three Projects Featuring K-Pop Supergroup BTS|url=https://www.hollywoodreporter.com/tv/tv-news/disney-to-produce-three-projects-featuring-k-pop-supergroup-bts-1235178553/|magazine=[[The Hollywood Reporter]]|last=Brzeski|first=Patrick|date=July 11, 2022|access-date=July 11, 2022|archive-date=July 12, 2022|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220712031910/https://www.hollywoodreporter.com/tv/tv-news/disney-to-produce-three-projects-featuring-k-pop-supergroup-bts-1235178553/|url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{cite magazine|title=Two BTS Series to Play on Disney Streaming Services Following Deal With Hybe|url=https://variety.com/2022/tv/news/bts-disney-streaming-hybe-1235314152/|magazine=[[Variety (magazine)|Variety]]|last=Frater|first=Patrick|date=July 11, 2022|access-date=July 11, 2022|archive-date=July 12, 2022|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220712030319/https://variety.com/2022/tv/news/bts-disney-streaming-hybe-1235314152/|url-status=live}}</ref>

Hybe became the largest shareholder of [[SM Entertainment]] in February 2023, after it acquired founder [[Lee Soo-man]]'s 14.8% stake in the company for approximately ₩422.8 billion.<ref>{{cite news|title=SM founder Lee Soo-man joins hands with Hybe to counter SM's Kakao partnership|url=https://www.koreaherald.com/view.php?ud=20230210000466|work=[[The Korea Herald]]|last=Hong|first=Yoo|date=February 10, 2023|access-date=February 10, 2023|archive-date=February 10, 2023|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230210051016/https://www.koreaherald.com/view.php?ud=20230210000466|url-status=live}}</ref> The company subsequently acquired [[Galaxia SM]]'s 1% stake on March 3.<ref>{{cite news|title=Galaxia SM sells its entire SM Entertainment stake to Hybe|url=https://pulsenews.co.kr/view.php?sc=30800028&year=2023&no=172196|work=[[Maeil Business Newspaper|Pulse News]]|last1=Oh|first1=Dae-seok|last2=Kim|first2=Minu|date=March 3, 2023|access-date=March 9, 2023|archive-date=March 9, 2023|archive-url=https://archive.today/20230309171754/https://pulsenews.co.kr/view.php?sc=30800028&year=2023&no=172196|url-status=live}}</ref> On March 12, Hybe announced that they no longer planned to own the majority stakes of SM Entertainment, said that the bidding war with [[Kakao]] could "damage shareholder value."<ref>{{cite web|title=K-Pop: HYBE and Kakao End Hostilities in Takeover Battle for SM Entertainment|url=https://variety.com/2023/music/news/k-pop-sm-entertainment-hybe-kakao-takeover-battle-1235550675/|website=[[Variety (magazine)|Variety]]|last=Frater|first=Patrick|date=March 11, 2023|access-date=March 12, 2023|archive-date=March 12, 2023|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230312182710/https://variety.com/2023/music/news/k-pop-sm-entertainment-hybe-kakao-takeover-battle-1235550675/|url-status=live}}</ref> On March 24, Hybe announced that it will sell its entire stake in SM Entertainment to Kakao by accepting its tender offer launched by Kakao.<ref>{{cite web|title=K-pop takeover battle loser HYBE to sell $437 mln stake in SM|url=https://www.reuters.com/markets/deals/south-koreas-hybe-sell-its-stake-sm-entertainment-2023-03-24/|website=[[Reuters]]|last=Kim|first=Hyunsu|date=March 24, 2023|access-date=March 24, 2023|archive-date=March 24, 2023|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230324041857/https://www.reuters.com/markets/deals/south-koreas-hybe-sell-its-stake-sm-entertainment-2023-03-24/|url-status=live}}</ref> However, on March 28, Hybe instead sold half of its stakes in SM to Kakao, leaving it only for 8.81%.<ref>{{cite news|title=Kakao's stake in K-pop agency SM climbs to 40%, HYBE retains 8.8%|url=https://www.reuters.com/business/media-telecom/kakao-reaches-40-stake-k-pop-agency-sm-hybe-keeps-88-2023-03-28/|work=Reuters|location=|last=Yim|first=Hyunsu|date=March 28, 2023|access-date=May 25, 2023|archive-date=April 5, 2023|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230405192535/https://www.reuters.com/business/media-telecom/kakao-reaches-40-stake-k-pop-agency-sm-hybe-keeps-88-2023-03-28/|url-status=live}}</ref> On May 27, 2025, Hybe announced it would sell all of its shares in SM Entertainment to [[Tencent Music]].<ref>{{Cite web|script-title=ko:하이브, SM 주식 전량 中 텐센트에 매각 '2천4백억 규모'|url=https://m.entertain.naver.com/now/article/382/0001199923|website=[[Naver]]|language=ko|access-date=May 27, 2025|archive-date=June 8, 2025|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250608061832/https://m.entertain.naver.com/now/article/382/0001199923|url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web|script-title=ko:하이브, SM 주식 전량 中 텐센트에 매각 '2천4백억 규모'|url=https://sports.donga.com/ent/article/all/20250527/131693635/1|website=스포츠동아|language=ko|date=May 27, 2025|access-date=May 27, 2025|archive-date=June 8, 2025|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250608061832/https://m.entertain.naver.com/now/article/382/0001199923|url-status=live}}</ref>

On May 23, 2023, Hybe signed a music distribution deal with Chinese streaming platform, [[Tencent Music]].<ref>{{cite news|title=South Korea's HYBE signs deal with China's Tencent Music|url=https://www.reuters.com/business/media-telecom/south-koreas-hybe-signs-deal-with-chinas-tencent-music-newspaper-2023-05-23/|work=Reuters|location=|last=Yim|first=Hyunsu|date=May 23, 2023|access-date=May 25, 2023|archive-date=May 25, 2023|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230525000856/https://www.reuters.com/business/media-telecom/south-koreas-hybe-signs-deal-with-chinas-tencent-music-newspaper-2023-05-23/|url-status=live}}</ref>

On August 10, 2023, Hybe announced its acquisition of the remaining 51.5% stake from CJ ENM in Belift Lab, making it as a wholly owned subsidiary.<ref name="hybefully">{{cite web|title=HYBE fully acquires K-pop agency Belift Lab from CJ ENM|url=https://koreajoongangdaily.joins.com/2023/08/10/business/industry/HYBE-Belift-Lab-CJ-ENM/20230810150855215.html|website=[[Korea JoongAng Daily]]|author1=Yoon So-yeon|date=August 10, 2023|access-date=August 10, 2023|archive-date=August 10, 2023|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230810232514/https://koreajoongangdaily.joins.com/2023/08/10/business/industry/HYBE-Belift-Lab-CJ-ENM/20230810150855215.html|url-status=live}}</ref><ref name="enhypenbacker">{{cite web|title=Enhypen-Backer Belift Lab Acquired by K-Pop Giant Hybe|url=https://variety.com/2023/music/news/k-pop-hybe-enhypen-belift-lab-1235692922/|website=[[Variety (magazine)|Variety]]|last=Frater|first=Patrick|date=August 10, 2023|access-date=August 10, 2023|archive-date=August 10, 2023|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230810082804/https://variety.com/2023/music/news/k-pop-hybe-enhypen-belift-lab-1235692922/|url-status=live}}</ref>

In November 2023, Hybe confirmed the establishment of its [[Latin music]] division, Hybe Latin America, based in [[Mexico City]], [[Los Angeles]], and [[Miami]].{{citation needed|date=May 2025}}

On August 27, 2024, Min Hee-jin was removed from her role as CEO and president of ADOR, but remained on the company's board of directors and continued producing for NewJeans. Kim Ju-young, Hybe's then-Chief Human Resources Officer and a member of HYBE's board, assumed the position of ADOR's CEO and president. Min objected this decision, describing her as a "grave violation of the shareholders' agreement."<ref>{{cite news|title=Min Hee-jin resigns as CEO of NewJeans' label|url=https://www.koreatimes.co.kr/www/nation/2024/08/398_381335.html|work=[[The Korea Times]]|date=August 27, 2024|archive-date=August 27, 2024|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240827081917/https://www.koreatimes.co.kr/www/nation/2024/08/398_381335.html|url-status=live}}</ref>

In December 2024, it was revealed that the Financial Supervisory Service (FSS) was investigating whether Chairman Bang Si-hyuk had entered into secret shareholder agreements with STIC Investments, Estone Equity Partners, and New Main Equity without informing other shareholders or the financial regulator. These agreements allegedly allowed him to financially benefit misleading other shareholders into believing that Hybe would not pursue an IPO.<ref>{{Cite web|title=HYBE and Chairman Bang Si-hyuk under investigation by South Korea's financial regulator over 'secret' shareholder contracts in IPO (report)|url=https://www.musicbusinessworldwide.com/hybe-and-chairman-bang-si-hyuk-under-investigation-by-south-koreas-financial-regulator-over-secret-shareholder-contracts-in-ipo-report/|website=Music Business Worldwide|date=December 2, 2024|access-date=May 28, 2025|archive-date=December 2, 2024|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20241202162035/https://www.musicbusinessworldwide.com/hybe-and-chairman-bang-si-hyuk-under-investigation-by-south-koreas-financial-regulator-over-secret-shareholder-contracts-in-ipo-report/|url-status=live}}</ref> On May 28, 2025, the FSS referred Bang Si-hyuk to the prosecution after concluding that the investigation met the threshold for charges.<ref>{{Cite web|title=Financial watchdog to refer HYBE CEO to prosecution on fraud allegations|url=https://koreajoongangdaily.joins.com/news/2025-05-28/business/industry/Financial-watchdog-to-refer-HYBE-CEO-to-prosecution-on-fraud-allegations/2318002|website=[[Korea JoongAng Daily]]|date=May 28, 2025|access-date=May 28, 2025|archive-date=May 28, 2025|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250528220932/https://koreajoongangdaily.joins.com/news/2025-05-28/business/industry/Financial-watchdog-to-refer-HYBE-CEO-to-prosecution-on-fraud-allegations/2318002|url-status=live}}</ref> The following day, Hybe's headquarters were searched in connection with allegations of insider trading by a former employee who allegedly used confidential information to profit from acquiring shares in YG Plus, based on prior knowledge of Hybe's planned investment in the company in January 2021.<ref>{{Cite web|title=Prosecutors investigate HYBE employee for insider trading, search headquarters|url=https://biz.chosun.com/en/en-society/2025/05/29/QBIAMZVU5JBU3CUG6JMNLYXP2U/|website=[[ChosunBiz]]|last=CHOSUNBIZ|date=May 29, 2025|access-date=May 29, 2025|archive-date=September 15, 2025|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250915023110/https://biz.chosun.com/en/en-society/2025/05/29/QBIAMZVU5JBU3CUG6JMNLYXP2U/|url-status=live}}</ref>

On May 28, 2025, Hybe announced the sale of Big Machine Rock to Gebbia Media,<ref>{{Cite web|title=HYBE America sells Big Machine Rock label to Gebbia Media|url=https://www.musicbusinessworldwide.com/hybe-america-sells-big-machine-rock-label-to-gebbia-media-scott-borchetta-to-join-advisory-board-of-advisory-board-of-siebert-financial/|website=Music Business Worldwide|date=May 28, 2025|access-date=May 29, 2025|archive-date=September 15, 2025|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250915023131/https://www.musicbusinessworldwide.com/hybe-america-sells-big-machine-rock-label-to-gebbia-media-scott-borchetta-to-join-advisory-board-of-advisory-board-of-siebert-financial/|url-status=live}}</ref> followed the next day by the announcement of establishing a Chinese subsidiary.<ref>{{Cite web|title=HYBE launches subsidiary in China, as K-Pop giant's global expansion continues|url=https://www.musicbusinessworldwide.com/hybe-launches-in-china-as-k-pop-powerhouses-global-expansion-continues/|website=Music Business Worldwide|date=May 29, 2025|access-date=June 4, 2025|archive-date=July 13, 2025|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250713204927/https://www.musicbusinessworldwide.com/hybe-launches-in-china-as-k-pop-powerhouses-global-expansion-continues/|url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web|title=HYBE China opens in Beijing as K-pop powerhouse aims to boost presence in local scene|url=https://koreajoongangdaily.joins.com/news/2025-05-29/business/industry/HYBE-China-opens-in-Beijing-as-Kpop-powerhouse-aims-to-boost-presence-in-local-scene/2318504|website=[[Korea JoongAng Daily]]|date=May 29, 2025|access-date=June 4, 2025|archive-date=May 29, 2025|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250529064359/https://koreajoongangdaily.joins.com/news/2025-05-29/business/industry/HYBE-China-opens-in-Beijing-as-Kpop-powerhouse-aims-to-boost-presence-in-local-scene/2318504|url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web|title=HYBE establishes Chinese unit to bolster advance of its artists - The Korea Times|url=https://www.koreatimes.co.kr/business/companies/20250529/hybe-establishes-chinese-unit-to-bolster-advance-of-its-artists|website=[[The Korea Times]]|date=May 29, 2025|access-date=June 4, 2025|archive-date=June 1, 2025|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250601114909/https://www.koreatimes.co.kr/business/companies/20250529/hybe-establishes-chinese-unit-to-bolster-advance-of-its-artists|url-status=live}}</ref>

On July 1, Hybe announced plans to expand its operations into India,<ref>{{Cite web|title=HYBE confirms 'strategic expansion' into India – in second half of 2025|url=https://www.musicbusinessworldwide.com/hybe-confirms-expansion-into-india-driven-by-chairman-bang-si-hyuks-vision-to-export-k-pop-methodologies/|website=Music Business Worldwide|last=Dalugdug|first=Mandy|date=July 1, 2025|access-date=September 26, 2025}}</ref> with the subsidiary establish on September 23.<ref>{{Cite web|title=BTS' agency eyes India's 1.4 billion market with new local branch|url=https://www.koreaherald.com/article/10581703|website=[[The Korea Herald]]|last=Jae-heun|first=Kim|date=September 23, 2025|access-date=September 26, 2025}}</ref> In the same month, one of Hybe's largest shareholders, [[Netmarble]] considered issuing approximately ₩2,500 billion ($2.5 trillion) in exchangeable bonds (EB), using part of its stake in Hybe as collateral to secure liquidity for repaying loans incurred during its acquisition of the U.S. social casino game company SpinX. While a formal underwriting agreement had not yet been signed, NH Investment & Securities emerged as the likely arranger, and NH Hedge Asset Management was reportedly forming a project fund with institutional investors to support the deal. Netmarble has previously utilized its Hybe holdings for financing, including a 2024 price return swap involving 2.6% equity and a 2023 block trade of approximately 6% of its shares.<ref>{{cite news|script-title=ko:'하이브 지분 활용' 넷마블, 2500억 EB 발행 추진|url=https://www.thebell.co.kr/free/Content/ArticleView.asp?key=202507111015182360102028|publisher=thebell|date=July 11, 2025|archive-date=August 12, 2025|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250812182636/https://www.thebell.co.kr/free/Content/ArticleView.asp?key=202507111015182360102028|url-status=live}}</ref> However, by August, Netmarble abandoned its plan, a decision attributed to the volatility and sharp decline in Hybe's stock price caused by an "owner risk" scandal involving Hybe's founder and Chairman Bang Si‑hyuk. The funding was intended to pay down debt from Netmarble's acquisition of SpinX (approximately ₩1.6 trillion in debt), which is now reduced to mid‑₩200 billion.<ref>{{cite news|script-title=ko:'하이브 주가 급락' 넷마블, 2500억 EB 발행 계획 철회|url=https://m.thebell.co.kr/m/newsview.asp?newskey=202508191538084600107554|publisher=thebell|date=August 20, 2025|archive-date=September 15, 2025|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250915023443/https://m.thebell.co.kr/m/newsview.asp?newskey=202508191538084600107554|url-status=live}}</ref>

On August 20, Mirae Asset Securities was preparing to sell the remaining portion of Hybe convertible bonds (CBs) worth approximately 1 trillion KRW, capitalizing on the recent surge in Hybe's stock price, which had risen to around 280,000 KRW—well above the CB conversion price of 218,000 KRW. The CBs, issued in October 2024 as zero-coupon, zero-interest bonds, were initially underwritten mainly by Mirae Asset, but some remained unsold due to concerns over the deal's structure and perceived favoritism. With Hybe's stock boosted by positive market sentiment, including anticipation of BTS's return and the easing of Chinese content restrictions, Mirae Asset sees a lucrative opportunity to offload the remaining CBs at a premium, maximizing returns through both underwriting fees and capital gains.<ref>{{Cite web|script-title=ko:미래에셋증권, '하이브 CB' 잔여물량 처분 나선다|url=https://www.thebell.co.kr/free/content/ArticleView.asp?key=202508181046575240106042|website=더벨뉴스|language=ko|access-date=August 24, 2025|archive-date=September 8, 2025|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250908131225/https://www.thebell.co.kr/free/content/ArticleView.asp?key=202508181046575240106042|url-status=live}}</ref> The same day, Hybe subsidiary ADOR appointed Lee Do-kyung as its new president. This leadership change follows the dismissal of former president Min Hee-jin in 2024 and a period of interim leadership under Kim Joo-young, who focused on stabilizing the company and restructuring operations. With that process complete, Hybe determined that Lee—who has been at Hybe since 2019 and has experience in strategic planning and IP projects—was the right person to lead ADOR into its next phase.<ref>{{Cite web|script-title=ko:어도어, 이도경 신임 대표 선임..."민희진 여파 정상화 판단"|url=https://news.kbs.co.kr/news/pc/view/view.do?ncd=8334993|website=KBS 뉴스|language=ko|access-date=August 22, 2025|archive-date=August 20, 2025|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250820075019/https://news.kbs.co.kr/news/pc/view/view.do?ncd=8334993|url-status=live}}</ref>

On December 15, Hybe announced a new global management partnership with Brandon Hixon and Colin Gayle, the co-managers of South African artist [[Tyla]], to develop Africa-based artists for international markets.<ref>{{Cite web|title=HYBE 'to build a global platform for African talent' via new partnership with Tyla managers Brandon Hixon and Colin Gayle|url=https://www.musicbusinessworldwide.com/hybe-to-build-a-global-platform-for-african-talent-via-new-partnership-with-tyla-managers-brandon-hixon-and-colin-gayle/|website=Music Business Worldwide|last=Stassen|first=Murray|date=December 15, 2025|access-date=January 7, 2026}}</ref>

In May 2026, Hybe unveiled a new corporate identity and updated mission statement, introducing "Discover a New Universe, Unlock an Immersive Journey" as its official mission, while retaining its previous music-focused principle, "We believe in Music", for internal use. The new branding emphasizes immersive fan experiences across content and services, reflecting a shift toward leveraging technology in its entertainment offerings. The company also adjusted its logo usage, allowing the wordmark and its derived "H" symbol to be used independently.<ref>{{Cite web|title=HYBE overhauls brand identity as it pushes further into superfans and technology|url=https://www.musicbusinessworldwide.com/hybe-overhauls-brand-identity-as-it-pushes-further-into-superfans-and-technology/|website=Music Business Worldwide|language=en-US|last=Dalugdug|first=Mandy|date=May 13, 2026|access-date=May 13, 2026}}</ref> On May 15, Hybe announced that former CHRO, Kim Joo-young, left the company after a recent filing revealed her shares, along with former CEO of Hybe America, Scooter Braun, and former CEO of Hybe Latin America, Kah Jong-hyun, dropped to zero,<ref>{{Cite web |last=기자 |first=박호민 |date=2026-05-15 |title=민희진 대표 후임 김주영 전 어도어 대표, 하이브 떠났다 |url=https://ilyo.co.kr/?ac=article_view&entry_id=510876 |access-date=2026-04-01 |website=일요신문 |language=ko}}</ref><ref name="ShareDump">{{Cite web |title=April 1, 2026 filing - DART - Repository of Korea's Corporate Filings |url=https://englishdart.fss.or.kr/dsbh001/main.do?rcpNo=20260401004602 |access-date=2026-04-01 |website=englishdart.fss.or.kr}}</ref> with Braun's own departure later revealed on July 1st.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Stassen |first=Murray |date=2026-07-01 |title=Scooter Braun resigned from HYBE's board – but the K-pop giant says he'll stick around as an advisor |url=https://www.musicbusinessworldwide.com/scooter-braun-resigned-from-hybes-board-but-the-k-pop-giant-says-hell-stick-around-as-an-advisor/ |access-date=2026-07-05 |website=Music Business Worldwide |language=en-US}}</ref>

On June 25, it was announced a joint partnership between HYBE's NEB division and INCS toenter would debut a girl group called Girls Archives, with their pre-debut song releasing on August 7th, in which NEB will provide concept design and storytelling for the group.<ref>{{Cite web |date=2026-06-23 |title=世界標準のストーリーテリング集団と映画『超かぐや姫！』の劇中歌を手がけるネットカルチャーの旗手が仕掛ける、リアル×バーチャル日韓ガールズグループ「Girls Archives.」始動 |url=https://prtimes.jp/main/html/rd/p/000000020.000125209.html |access-date=2026-07-05 |website=プレスリリース・ニュースリリース配信シェアNo.1｜PR TIMES |language=ja-JP}}</ref>

On July 23, HYBE disclosed that its subsidiary Supertone had been approved for dissolution following a resolution adopted at an extraordinary shareholders' meeting on July 15th.<ref>{{Cite web|title=Supertone - DART - Repository of Korea's Corporate Filings|url=https://englishdart.fss.or.kr/dsbh001/main.do?rcpNo=20260723000105|website=englishdart.fss.or.kr|access-date=July 23, 2026}}</ref> Although the corporate entity is being dissolved, HYBE announced that parts of Supertone's business would continue under a new operating company. Its AI voice services Shift, Clear, and Air were to be transferred to a new company and continue without interruption, with existing licences and purchased voices remaining valid. Meanwhile, Play, the Supertone API, and Voice Builder were discontinued.<ref>{{Cite web |title=Supertone {{!}} 보이스 인텔리전스 플랫폼 & AI 음성 기술 |url=https://www.supertone.ai/ko |access-date=2026-07-26 |website=www.supertone.ai |language=ko}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |last=이영호 |date=2026-07-26 |title=하이브, 또 M&A 잔혹사…이번엔 음성AI 수퍼톤 청산 수순 |url=https://www.sedaily.com/article/20071910 |access-date=2026-07-26 |website=서울경제 |language=ko}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |title=하이브, 또 M&A 잔혹사…이번엔 음성AI 수퍼톤 청산 수순 [시그널] |url=https://news.nate.com/view/20260726n12041 |access-date=2026-07-26 |website=네이트 뉴스 |language=ko}}</ref>

==Company value and investments==
Big Hit Entertainment initially operated as a private company with Bang Si-hyuk as the largest shareholder of its stock.<ref name=":3">{{cite web|title=Big Hit Entertainment {{small|''("Who We Are" section)''}}|url=https://www.bighitcorp.com/eng/|access-date=June 23, 2020|archive-date=June 23, 2020|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200623185711/https://www.bighitcorp.com/eng/|url-status=live}}</ref> In 2018, the Korean mobile gaming company [[Netmarble Games|Netmarble]] acquired the second largest percentage of the company, paying a reported ₩201.4&nbsp;billion ($191.8&nbsp;million) for 25.71%.<ref>{{cite web|title=Game maker becomes second largest shareholder of BTS' agency|url=http://www.ajudaily.com/view/20180404164806895|work=[[Aju Business Daily]]|last=Kim|first=Chang-won|date=April 4, 2018|access-date=February 17, 2021|archive-date=April 6, 2018|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180406145058/http://www.ajudaily.com/view/20180404164806895|url-status=live}}</ref> In October 2018, private investment firm [[STIC Investments]] received an undisclosed share of Big Hit for an investment of roughly ₩104&nbsp;billion ($93&nbsp;million).<ref>{{cite web|script-title=ko:스틱인베스트, BTS에 1040억 베팅|url=http://www.paxnet.co.kr/news/mainView?vNewsSetId=5617&articleId=2018101111304401641&wlog_nsise=popularnews|website=Paxnet News|language=ko|last=Kwon|first=Il-woon|date=November 10, 2018|access-date=February 17, 2021|archive-date=August 3, 2019|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190803094321/http://www.paxnet.co.kr/news/mainView?vNewsSetId=5617&articleId=2018101111304401641&wlog_nsise=popularnews|url-status=live}}</ref>

In 2007, Big Hit had four employees and was near bankruptcy, but Bang was able to keep the company afloat after the local success of [[8Eight]]'s ''Without a Heart'' in 2009.<ref>{{cite web|title=Mogul Behind K-Pop Boy Band BTS Considers IPO|url=https://www.bloomberg.com/news/articles/2017-12-17/mogul-behind-world-s-most-tweeted-about-boy-band-considers-ipo|website=[[Bloomberg News|Bloomberg]]|last=Kim|first=Sohee|date=December 17, 2017|access-date=February 17, 2021|archive-date=December 23, 2017|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20171223200533/https://www.bloomberg.com/news/articles/2017-12-17/mogul-behind-world-s-most-tweeted-about-boy-band-considers-ipo|url-status=live}}</ref> Over the years, its value rose in tandem with the increasing global popularity of its first boy group BTS.<ref>{{cite web|title=BTS label Big Hit Entertainment joins the ranks of unicorns: HRI report|url=https://pulsenews.co.kr/view.php?year=2019&no=392592|website=Pulse News|last1=Kim|first1=Yeon-joo|last2=Kim|first2=Hyo-jin|date=June 7, 2019|access-date=December 11, 2020|archive-date=September 27, 2020|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200927142724/https://pulsenews.co.kr/view.php?year=2019&no=392592|url-status=live}}</ref> In March 2018, Big Hit's earnings were publicized for the first time. The company reported revenue of ₩92.4&nbsp;billion ($82&nbsp;million) and operating profit of ₩32.5&nbsp;billion ($29&nbsp;million) for 2017.<ref>{{cite web|title=Expectations high for BTS-backed Big Hit Entertainment's IPO|url=http://www.theinvestor.co.kr/view.php?ud=20180326000769|via=[[The Korea Herald]]|work=The Investor|last=Lee|first=Ji-Yoo|date=March 26, 2018|access-date=August 3, 2019|archive-date=November 7, 2018|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20181107104354/http://www.theinvestor.co.kr/view.php?ud=20180326000769|url-status=live}}</ref><ref name="theinvestor.co.kr">{{cite web|title=STIC Investments invests W104b in Big Hit|url=http://www.theinvestor.co.kr/view.php?ud=20181011000694|work=The Investor|date=October 11, 2018|access-date=December 11, 2020|archive-date=November 7, 2018|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20181107145121/http://www.theinvestor.co.kr/view.php?ud=20181011000694|url-status=live}}</ref> Initial estimates valued Big Hit at up to ₩700&nbsp;billion ($624&nbsp;million) if entered into an [[Initial public offering|IPO]] that very month, making CEO Bang the richest in the South Korean entertainment business due to his large shareholding position, and leaving him with personal ownership value of roughly ₩350&nbsp;billion ($314&nbsp;million).<ref>{{cite web|script-title=ko:양현석 넘은 박진영... BTS 대박에 방시혁 1위|trans-title=Park Jin-young over Yang Hyun-seok... Bang Si-hyuk is No. 1 thanks to BTS.|url=https://www.hankyung.com/finance/article/2018032749871|work=[[The Korea Economic Daily]]|language=ko|last=Choi|first=Man-soo|date=March 27, 2018|access-date=August 3, 2019|archive-date=July 29, 2019|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190729143619/https://www.hankyung.com/finance/article/2018032749871|url-status=live}}</ref> By October 2018, the company was valued at more than ₩1&nbsp;trillion.<ref name="theinvestor.co.kr" />

Big Hit's success has been attributed to its innovative management style, more like an IT company than an entertainment company, which has become the new standard in the K-pop industry; it includes a wide usage of [[social media]] to capture people's interest and transform it into sales, the creation of artist-related multimedia content, and the full use of [[fandom]] energy.<ref>{{cite web|title=Big Hit Entertainment│① "New Generation" to introduce a new future of K-pop|url=https://www.ize.co.kr/articleView.html?no=2019040102337250921|website=Ize|last1=Kang|first1=Myoung Seok|last2=Yu|first2=Jeon Rim|last3=Sim|first3=Gukby|date=April 1, 2019|access-date=December 11, 2020|archive-date=April 5, 2019|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190405084616/https://www.ize.co.kr/articleView.html?no=2019040102337250921|url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{cite web|title=Big Hit Entertainment│② The Decisions that Made Big Hit|url=https://www.ize.co.kr/articleView.html?no=2019040102317286351|website=Ize|last1=Park|first1=Hee A|last2=Sim|first2=Gukby|date=April 1, 2019|access-date=December 11, 2020|archive-date=April 5, 2019|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190405100528/https://www.ize.co.kr/articleView.html?no=2019040102317286351|url-status=live}}</ref> Financials published in March 2019 for 2018, showed a 132% increase in sales compared to 2017, with Big Hit making approximately {{KRWConvert|214.2|b}}. Operating profit rose 97% to {{KRWConvert|64.1|b}}, while net profit rose 105% to {{KRWConvert|50.2|b}}.<ref>{{cite magazine|title=BTS Helps Label Big Hit Entertainment to Record Result In 2018|url=https://www.billboard.com/articles/news/bts/8503273/bts-label-big-hit-entertainment-record-results-2018|magazine=[[Billboard (magazine)|Billboard]]|last=Herman|first=Tamar|date=March 20, 2019|access-date=February 17, 2021|archive-date=March 21, 2019|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190321151211/https://www.billboard.com/articles/news/bts/8503273/bts-label-big-hit-entertainment-record-results-2018|url-status=live}}</ref> By June 2019, the company was valued between {{KRWConvert|1.28|t}} and {{KRWConvert|2.22|t}}<ref>{{cite web|script-title=ko:BTS 소속사 '빅히트엔터' 유니콘 됐다|trans-title=BTS' agency 'Big Hit Entertainment' became a unicorn|url=https://www.mk.co.kr/news/economy/view/2019/06/390669/|work=[[Maeil Business Newspaper|Maeil Business News]]|language=ko|last=Kim|first=Yeon-joo|date=June 6, 2019|access-date=February 17, 2021|archive-date=June 8, 2019|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190608113358/https://mk.co.kr/news/economy/view/2019/06/390669/|url-status=live}}</ref> As of March 2020, its total value was roughly {{KRWConvert|6|t}}.<ref>{{cite web|script-title=ko:[과열로 치닫는 '빅히트' 몸값] 3조원에서 6조원으로 2배 '껑충'|trans-title=['Big Hit's value jumped] Double the price from 3 trillion won to 6 trillion won.|url=https://n.news.naver.com/article/243/0000009666|via=[[Naver|Naver News]]|work=[[JoongAng Ilbo|JoongAng Ilbo Economist]]|language=ko|last=Bae|first=Dong-ju|date=March 2, 2020|access-date=February 17, 2021|archive-date=April 7, 2020|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200407014700/https://n.news.naver.com/article/243/0000009666|url-status=live}}</ref> Big Hit commenced plans to go public on May 21, and applied for a pre-IPO consultation with the [[Korea Exchange]]. This is required under South Korean law before a company can file paperwork for an IPO.<ref>{{cite web|title=BTS agency Big Hit may file for IPO as early as this week|url=https://pulsenews.co.kr/view.php?year=2020&no=542320|website=Pulse News|last=<!--Staff|first=writers; no by-line-->|date=May 27, 2020|access-date=February 17, 2021|archive-date=June 6, 2020|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200606131726/https://pulsenews.co.kr/view.php?year=2020&no=542320|url-status=live}}</ref> One week later, the company filed for a preliminary review of their planned IPO.<ref>{{cite web|title=BTS agency Big Hit applies for preliminary review for listing|url=http://www.koreaherald.com/view.php?ud=20200528000911|via=[[The Korea Herald]]|agency=[[Yonhap News Agency|Yonhap]]|date=May 29, 2020|access-date=May 29, 2020|archive-date=May 28, 2020|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200528185004/http://www.koreaherald.com/view.php?ud=20200528000911|url-status=live}}</ref> On October 15, Big Hit was listed in the [[KOSPI]] index and began trading stocks.<ref>{{cite web|script-title=ko:빅히트, 시초가 27만원으로 코스피 입성.."세계적으로 사랑받는 기업되겠다" [종합]|trans-title=|url=https://entertain.naver.com/read?oid=109&aid=0004290141|via=[[Naver]]|work=Osen|language=ko|last=Ji|first=Min-kyung|date=October 15, 2020|access-date=February 17, 2021|archive-date=October 17, 2020|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20201017051530/https://entertain.naver.com/read?oid=109&aid=0004290141|url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{cite magazine|title=BTS Label Big Hit Entertainment Doubles Shares in Smash Stock Market Debut|url=https://www.billboard.com/articles/business/9466518/bts-big-hit-entertainment-ipo-stock-market-debut-share-price|magazine=Billboard|last=Cirisano|first=Tatiana|date=October 15, 2020|access-date=February 17, 2021|archive-date=October 15, 2020|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20201015211716/https://www.billboard.com/articles/business/9466518/bts-big-hit-entertainment-ipo-stock-market-debut-share-price|url-status=live}}</ref>

Per the company's 2020 annual report, its first since going public, Big Hit  recorded net income of {{KRWConvert|86.2|b}}, a 19.1% increase compared to 2019's {{KRWConvert|72.4|b}}<ref name="yonhap">{{cite web|title=Big Hit Entertainment 2020 net profit up 19.1 pct. to 86.2 bln won|url=https://en.yna.co.kr/view/AEN20210223007900320|work=[[Yonhap News Agency]]|author1=영문경제|date=February 23, 2021|access-date=February 26, 2021|archive-date=February 26, 2021|archive-url=https://archive.today/20210226222528/https://en.yna.co.kr/view/AEN20210223007900320|url-status=live}}</ref> Operating profit rose 44.3% to ₩142.4 billion, while sales increased 35.6% to ₩796.3 billion<ref name="yonhap" />—bolstered in part by album revenue and various acquisitions—with the company experiencing its best quarterly performance yet in the fourth quarter of 2020.<ref name="BH 2020">{{cite web|title=Big Hit's 2020 net soars amid unrelenting BTS popularity|url=http://www.koreaherald.com/view.php?ud=20210223000920|work=[[The Korea Herald]]|date=February 23, 2021|access-date=February 26, 2021|archive-date=February 26, 2021|archive-url=https://archive.today/20210226231939/http://www.koreaherald.com/view.php?ud=20210223000920|url-status=live}}</ref> Though concert revenue decreased in the wake of the [[COVID-19 pandemic in South Korea|COVID-19 pandemic]], merchandise, content, and fan club-related revenue increased 53%, 71%, and 66%, respectively.<ref name="BH 2020" /><ref>{{cite web|title=Big Hit posts record earnings in '20 on BTS feat, stock nears debut price|url=https://www.mk.co.kr/news/english/view/2021/02/180925/|work=Maeil Business News|last1=Kang|first1=Young-woon|last2=Lee|first2=Soo-min|date=February 24, 2021|access-date=February 26, 2021|archive-date=February 26, 2021|archive-url=https://archive.today/20210226234817/https://www.mk.co.kr/news/english/view/2021/02/180925/|url-status=live}}</ref> In 2021, following its rebranding as Hybe, the company became the first South Korean music agency to surpass the {{KRWConvert|1|t}} benchmark in annual sales. It recorded annual revenue of {{KRWConvert|1.2|t}} operating profit of {{KRWConvert|190.3|b}}, and net income of {{KRWConvert|141|t}}.<ref>{{cite web|title=(LEAD) Hybe tops 1 tln won in annual sales, first in K-pop industry|url=https://en.yna.co.kr/view/AEN20220222009851320|work=[[Yonhap News Agency]]|last=Shim|first=S.|date=February 22, 2022|access-date=February 23, 2022|archive-date=February 22, 2022|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220222100649/https://en.yna.co.kr/view/AEN20220222009851320|url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{cite web|title=HYBE had a great 2021 despite fewer concerts|url=https://koreajoongangdaily.joins.com/2022/02/22/business/industry/HYBE-revenue-profit/20220222180939646.html|work=[[Korea JoongAng Daily]]|last=Soon|first=So-yeon|date=February 22, 2022|access-date=February 23, 2022|archive-date=February 22, 2022|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220222093422/https://koreajoongangdaily.joins.com/2022/02/22/business/industry/HYBE-revenue-profit/20220222180939646.html|url-status=live}}</ref>

==Corporate structure, governance, and significant holdings==
===Affiliate structure and governance disclosures===
According to HYBE's 2026 large-scale business group disclosure filed with the Korea Fair Trade Commission, the group consisted of 77 domestic and overseas affiliates as of May 2026, including operations in South Korea, Japan, China, the United States, Mexico, India, and Singapore.<ref name="LSBD2026">{{Cite web |title=2026 Large-scale Business Disclosure - DART - Repository of Korea's Corporate Filings|url=https://englishdart.fss.or.kr/dsbh001/main.do?rcpNo=20260528000777 |access-date=2026-05-28 |website=English DART}}</ref>

The filing confirmed the establishment of several new affiliates during the reporting period, including Hybe India Entertainment Private Limited, Jconic Inc., NFO, LLC, Truvatos S de RL de CV, and Pase A La Fama S de RL de CV. Hybe also established ABD Co., Ltd. in March 2026 as a new Korean music label subsidiary led by former Pledis executive Noh Ji-won.<ref name="LSBD2026"/>

Hybe disclosed that its domestic non-financial affiliates employed a combined total of 2,305 workers. The largest domestic subsidiaries by headcount were Hybe (908 employees), Weverse Company (436), Drimage (311), BigHit Music (174), and Pledis Entertainment (143).<ref name="LSBD2026"/>

The report also detailed changes in HYBE's overseas structure. Hybe IM Japan Inc. was renamed Drimage Japan Inc., while Xing Can Sheng Shi (Beijing) Entertainment Co., Ltd. was renamed Pledis China Co., Ltd.<ref name="LSBD2026"/>

===Board and executive changes===
The 2026 disclosure expanded on HYBE's board composition and governance structure. New internal director appointments included Isaac Lee, CEO and chairman of Hybe America, and Kevin Mayer, co-CEO of Candle Media and former CEO of TikTok.<ref name="FY2026_Q1" />

Additional outside directors appointed in 2025–2026 included economist Lee Sang-seung, law professor Cho Won-kyung, former vice minister Baek Seung-joo, and finance executive Han Chang-soo.<ref name="LSBD2026"/>

The filing also documented executives across Hybe subsidiaries, including:
*Shin Sun-jung as CEO/president of BigHit Music
*Kim Yeon-soo as CEO/president of Pledis Entertainment
*Kim Joo-young as CEO/president of Source Music
*Kim Tae-ho as CEO of Belift Lab
*Lee Do-kyung as CEO/president of ADOR
*Lee Chang-woo as CEO/president of KOZ Entertainment
*Choi Jun-won as CEO/president of Weverse Company
*Noh Ji-won as CEO/president of newly established ABD Co., Ltd.

Hybe further disclosed extensive interlocking executive positions among affiliates, particularly involving CFO Lee Kyung-joon, CLO Jung Jin-soo, and other senior executives serving concurrently on subsidiary boards.

===Financial and operational disclosures===
Hybe reported consolidated separate-basis assets of approximately ₩3.73 trillion and equity of ₩2.66 trillion for the parent company at the end of fiscal year 2025.<ref name="LSBD2026"/>

Among major subsidiaries:
*BigHit Music reported ₩437.5 billion in revenue and ₩47.9 billion in operating profit.
*Belift Lab reported ₩154.7 billion in revenue and ₩49.9 billion in operating profit.
*Pledis Entertainment reported ₩288.5 billion in revenue and ₩69.7 billion in operating profit.
*Weverse Company reported ₩299.7 billion in revenue and ₩2.0 billion in operating profit.

The disclosure additionally documented multiple board approvals during 2025–2026 involving restricted stock unit (RSU) grants, intercompany loans, acquisitions of shares in other corporations, subsidiary establishment approvals, and extensions of credit agreements.

===Significant holdings and executive ownership===
====Shareholding by reporting entity and related parties====
As of May 21, 2026, Bang Si-hyuk held 12,247,734 shares of HYBE, representing 28.42% of the company's voting shares. Netmarble Co., Ltd. held 3,050,813 shares (7.08%), and Dunamu Co., Ltd. held 2,302,570 shares (5.34%).<ref name="FY2026_Q1" /> The National Pension Service held 3,541,084 shares (8.2%) according to HYBE's earlier quarterly filing.<ref name="FY2026_Q1" />

Among current and former executives and related parties, CEO Lee Jae-sang held 32,040 shares (0.07%). Kim Tae-ho held 21,314 shares, Jung Jin-soo held 14,401 shares, and Han Hyun-rok held 16,468 shares. Lee Kyung-joon, Lee Da-hye, [[Han Sung-soo (music executive)|Han Sung-soo]], Lee Ga-jun, Choi Jun-won, Shin Sun-jung, and Jung Woo-yong each hold fewer than 5,000 shares.<ref name="FY2025" /> As of March 31, 2026, former executives Scott Samuel Braun, Kah Jong-hyun, and Kim Joo-young no longer held shares.<ref name="ShareDump" />

On May 20, 2026, Bang Si-hyuk donated 192,126 common shares of Hybe to the company for use in employee and executive compensation programs at Hybe and its affiliates. The shares were valued at {{KRWConvert|45.150|b}} based on the closing price before the board resolution date.<ref name="Gift2026_2">{{Cite web |title=Large-scale internal transaction disclosure (second donation) - DART - Repository of Korea's Corporate Filings|url=https://dart.fss.or.kr/dsaf001/main.do?rcpNo=20260521000564 |access-date=2026-05-21 |website=DART}}</ref>

An earlier donation of 546,120 shares valued at {{KRWConvert|209.164|b}} was made by Bang Si-hyuk on March 31, 2026 for the same purpose.<ref name="Gift2026">{{Cite web |title=Large-scale internal transaction disclosure (first donation) - DART - Repository of Korea's Corporate Filings|url=https://dart.fss.or.kr/dsaf001/main.do?rcpNo=20260429000563 |access-date=2026-05-14 |website=DART}}</ref>

====Contracts related to shares====
Bang Si-hyuk, Netmarble, and Dunamu are parties to shareholder agreements concerning the joint exercise of voting rights, including matters related to director appointments, mergers, and acquisitions.<ref name="FY2026_Q1" />

Bang Si-hyuk also pledged 640,000 shares as collateral for loans from [[Nonghyup Bank]]. The loans carried a weighted average interest rate of 3.8% and are scheduled from May 31, 2025 to March 31, 2027.<ref name="FY2026_Q1" />

Netmarble held exchangeable bonds linked to 528,576 Hybe shares. The bonds were issued on November 19, 2025 and mature on November 19, 2030.<ref name="FY2026_Q1" />

In total, 18,769,693 shares, representing 43.55% of HYBE's equity, were subject to contractual arrangements.<ref name="FY2026_Q1" />

====Executive and employee matters====
=====Board and executive composition=====
{{As of|2026|3|31}}, the board of directors comprised:
*Chairman: Bang Si-hyuk
*CEO: Lee Jae-sang
*Outside directors: Kim Byung-kyu, Park Young-ho, Lee Mi-kyung, Lee Sang-seung, Baek Seung-joo, and Jo Won-kyung

Unregistered executives included former COO Kim Tae-ho, CCO Park Tae-hee, CFO Lee Kyung-joon, HBS President Lee Seung-seok, CAMO Kim Shin-gyu, and CLO Jung Jin-soo.<ref name="FY2025" />

=====Executive remuneration=====
Shareholders approved a total remuneration limit of {{KRWConvert|5.0|b}} for nine registered directors. During the reporting period, seven directors and auditors received {{KRWConvert|1.972|b}}, averaging {{KRWConvert|282|m}} per person.<ref name="FY2025" />

Breakdown:
*Registered directors (excluding outside directors and audit committee members): {{KRWConvert|1.591|b}}
*Outside directors: {{KRWConvert|150|m}}
*Audit committee members: {{KRWConvert|231|m}}

Lee Jae-sang received {{KRWConvert|1.647|b}} in total compensation. The five highest-paid executives and directors were Lee Jae-sang, Park Tae-hee, Shin Young-jae, Lee Kyung-joon, and Jung Jin-soo.<ref name="FY2025" />

=====Stock-based compensation=====
Hybe operated a stock-based compensation program with stock options totaling {{KRWConvert|5.323|b}} for seven recipients. A restricted stock unit (RSU) program launched in 2024 distributed 158,640 shares in 2025 to 2,122 employees and executives. During 2025, 13,088 RSUs vested for 31 recipients, and 590 RSUs were canceled. As of December 31, 2025, 208,342 unvested RSUs remained outstanding.<ref name="FY2025" />

In April 2026, treasury stock bonuses were granted to several affiliated company executives, including Kim Tae-ho, Jung Jin-soo, Han Hyun-rok, Choi Jun-won, Shin Sun-jung, and Jung Woo-yong. Some of these individuals were subsequently added as specially related parties in HYBE's shareholding reports.<ref name="FY2026_Q1" />

==Pillars and subsidiaries==
{{Anchor|Hybe HQ}}
Hybe consists of three pillars: Music, Platform, and Technology. Under each division are wholly or partially owned subsidiaries of its parent corporation.<ref name="MBW Hybe presentation">{{cite web|title=BTS' agency changes name to HYBE|url=https://www.musicbusinessworldwide.com/bts-label-big-hit-entertainment-officially-rebrands-as-hybe/|work=Music Business Worldwide|last=Stassen|first=Murray|date=March 19, 2021|access-date=April 4, 2021|archive-date=April 2, 2021|archive-url=https://archive.today/20210402211256/https://www.musicbusinessworldwide.com/bts-label-big-hit-entertainment-officially-rebrands-as-hybe/|url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{cite web|title=BTS' agency changes name to HYBE|url=https://www.koreatimes.co.kr/www/art/2021/03/398_305775.html|work=[[The Korea Times]]|last=Park|first=Ji-won|date=March 21, 2021|access-date=April 3, 2021|archive-date=March 19, 2021|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210319102101/https://m.koreatimes.co.kr/pages/article.asp?newsIdx=305775|url-status=live}}</ref> {{As of|2026}}, the company has 85 subsidiaries in total.<ref name="FY2025" />

===Music===
{{Anchor|Hybe Labels}}
This is the entertainment and music production division. Formerly branded as Big Hit Labels and Hybe Labels, it was later scrapped as a brand as of May 2026, as a part of a wider rebranding of the company. Subsidiaries within the division are creatively and operationally independent, yet are overseen by either the CLO or CFO of Hybe as internal directors.<ref name="FY2025" />

