====Hybe America====
{{Anchor|Hybe America}}
Prior to the rebrand, the subsidiary was known as Big Hit America. Following the rebranding, Hybe bought over all ₩1.7&nbsp;trillion ($1.5&nbsp;billion) of Big Hit America's shares, making it a wholly owned subsidiary under the corporation, and it became Hybe America.<ref name="KJAD Ithaca" /> This was in preparation for Hybe's acquisition of Ithaca Holdings through Hybe America.{{Efn|name=Hybe America}} BH Odyssey Merger Sub was created as a subsidiary of Hybe America to facilitate its buyout of Ithaca. Once finalized, Ithaca would become a subsidiary of Hybe America, and BH Odyssey would be dissolved.<ref name="KJAD Ithaca" /><ref name="Variety Ithaca" /> The merger is assumed to have been completed in the first quarter of 2021 per quarterly earning reports published on May 14.<ref>{{cite web|title=Hybe Quarterly Report|url=https://dart.fss.or.kr/dsaf001/main.do?rcpNo=20210514001345|via=|website=dart.fss.or.kr|publisher=[[Financial Supervisory Service (South Korea)|Financial Supervisory Service]]|author=Hybe Corporation|date=May 14, 2021|access-date=July 15, 2021|archive-date=June 2, 2021|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210602212504/https://dart.fss.or.kr/dsaf001/main.do?rcpNo=20210514001345|url-status=live}}</ref>

In July 2021, Yoon and Braun were appointed co-CEOs of Hybe America, and jointly managed US operations. Yoon was responsible for "the localization of Hybe's K-pop business model in the US" and "oversee[ing] the training, production and marketing process through which new talent is discovered" while Braun focused "on solidifying Hybe's presence in the US music industry" and "cementing [the company's] competitiveness in the stateside market." Chief Strategy Officer (CSO) Jaesang Lee, who led the Ithaca acquisition, was made Chief Operations Officer (COO).<ref>{{cite web|title=Who are the key players at Hybe in the post-Bang Si-hyuk era?|url=http://www.koreaherald.com/view.php?ud=20210715001055|work=[[The Korea Herald]]|last=Choi|first=Ji-won|date=July 19, 2021|access-date=August 15, 2021|archive-date=July 19, 2021|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210719030935/http://www.koreaherald.com/view.php?ud=20210715001055|url-status=live}}</ref><ref name="BSH resign MBW" /> Plans for the launch of a new global girl group in collaboration with Geffen were announced on November 4; auditions closed on November 28.<ref name="NME 2021 briefing">{{cite web|title=BTS' agency HYBE announces plans to debut new groups in the US and Japan|url=https://www.nme.com/news/music/hybe-plans-debut-new-groups-us-japan-bts-video-game-nft-webtoon-enhypen-txt-3087092|website=[[NME]]|last=Chin|first=Carmen|date=November 4, 2021|access-date=November 4, 2021|archive-date=November 4, 2021|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20211104072704/https://www.nme.com/news/music/hybe-plans-debut-new-groups-us-japan-bts-video-game-nft-webtoon-enhypen-txt-3087092|url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{cite magazine|title=HYBE and Universal Music Launching Global Girl Group Audition Challenge|url=https://www.billboard.com/articles/news/9655156/hybe-universal-music-group-geffen-global-girl-group-audition|magazine=Billboard|last=Kaufman|first=Gil|date=November 4, 2021|access-date=November 4, 2021|archive-date=November 4, 2021|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20211104210355/https://www.billboard.com/articles/news/9655156/hybe-universal-music-group-geffen-global-girl-group-audition|url-status=live}}</ref> Braun became the sole CEO in January 2023,<ref>{{cite magazine|title=Scooter Braun Becomes Sole CEO of HYBE America|url=https://variety.com/2023/music/news/scooter-braun-hybe-ceo-bts-1235500913/|magazine=[[Variety (magazine)|Variety]]|last1=Shafer|first1=Ellise|last2=Frater|first2=Patrick|date=January 25, 2023|access-date=January 25, 2023|archive-date=January 25, 2023|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230125172820/https://variety.com/2023/music/news/scooter-braun-hybe-ceo-bts-1235500913/|url-status=live}}</ref> subsequently leading the acquisition of the American entertainment company QC Media Holdings in February.<ref>{{Cite web|title=HYBE America Acquires Quality Control, Home to Lil Baby, Migos and City Girls, in $300 Million-Plus Deal Led by Scooter Braun|url=https://variety.com/2023/music/global/hybe-america-quality-control-deal-1235517252/|website=[[Variety (magazine)|Variety]]|last1=Aswad|first1=Jem|last2=Halperin|first2=Shirley|date=February 9, 2023|access-date=February 10, 2023|archive-date=February 10, 2023|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230210022250/https://variety.com/2023/music/global/hybe-america-quality-control-deal-1235517252/|url-status=live}}</ref> In July 2025, Braun transitioned out of his role as CEO of Hybe America. Isaac Lee, Chairman of Hybe Latin America, took Braun's place.<ref>{{Cite web|title=Scooter Braun "transitions" from CEO of HYBE America|url=https://www.nme.com/news/music/scooter-braun-is-stepping-down-as-ceo-of-hybe-america-3875406|website=[[NME]]|last=Dunworth|first=Liberty|date=July 2, 2025|access-date=September 13, 2025|archive-date=July 7, 2025|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250707094751/https://www.nme.com/news/music/scooter-braun-is-stepping-down-as-ceo-of-hybe-america-3875406|url-status=live}}</ref>

